\subsubsection{Theoretical and Technical Developments Regarding {MC} Channel Models}

{MC} channel models are a widespread research topic in the scientific community across different scales: {(i)} Human vessels, organs, and tissues on a larger scale; {(ii)} on the cellular scale, cell-to-cell communication and information processing; and {(iii)} on the molecular scale-free diffusion or junction {MC} channels between cells; see~\cite{akyildiz2019moving}.
These theoretical models underpin the feasibility of applications such as drug delivery, disease monitoring (e.g., cancer initiation and progression), and the deployment of body network infrastructures \cite{lu2020wireless}.

Theoretical developments in \cite{jamali2019channel} summarize the end-to-end \ac{CIR} derivation for various {MC} geometries at the molecular scale.
{MC} channels include free diffusion, advection, and chemical degradation channels, see~\cite[Table 1]{jamali2019channel}.
At the tissue scale, mobility models are summarized in \cite{nakano2019methods} for passive and active bionanomachines that carry information.
Passive mobility models include random walks in a fluid and with external forces, whereas active mobility models exhibit a bias towards a preferred direction, as seen in the case of chemotaxis.\footnote{The chemotaxis process describes the mobility of bacteria towards the higher concentration of attractant molecules, see models in \cite{caferzade2023modeling,burggraf2022modeling}.}
Besides, theoretical works also target more functional problems in {MC} links.
Examples include methods for channel parameter estimation \cite{huang2022survey}, modulation and demodulation \cite{kuran2021survey,gursoy2022towards}, coding \cite{hofmann2023coding}, and the formulation of spatial domain resources through \ac{MIMO} schemes as in~\cite{koo2021mimo}.
These works illustrate the capabilities of actual communication schemes at the nanoscale.

Not only does theoretical work drive the study of {MC} links, but also the development of simulators; see \cite[Sec. V.A]{jamali2019channel}, \cite[Sec. II.5]{lu2020wireless}).
Surveys also illustrate experimental testbeds as tracks of technological readiness in the near future, see \cite{lotter2023experimental,lotter2023experimental2,kuscu2019transmitter}, \cite[Sec. V.B]{jamali2019channel}, \cite[IV B.2]{yang2020comprehensive}, and \cite[Sec. IV]{akyildiz2019moving}.
This research field focuses on interfaces between the biological and electrical domains, such as synaptic links in \cite{veletic2019synaptic}, optical links in \cite{jornet2019optogenomic}, electrical domain connections in \cite{kim2019redox}, and \ac{RF} signals in the \si{\tera\hertz} domain, as described in \cite{yang2020comprehensive}.

