\subsubsection{Environments for MC Channel Estimation}

In the literature, {NN} models for channel estimation and modeling have been deployed in two main {MC} environments: In simulated free-diffusion {MC} channels as in \cite{yilmaz2017machine,lee2017machine,ozdemir2021estimating,ozdemir2024estimating,cheng2024channel,damrath2018array,cheng2025deep,ozbey2024artificial}, within the network of the \ac{HCS} as in \cite{mohamed2021biocyber}, and in realworld testbeds for open air channels, as in \cite{gulec2020distance}.


\noindent
\textbf{Free-diffusion environments:} The more commonplace environment of free diffusion poses challenges in evaluating the channel model for complex topologies.
Examples include scenarios where multiple senders, reflectors, and absorbers are situated between the emitter and the receiver, which hinder the analytical development of closed-form solutions for the {CIR}; see \Cref{fig_free_diffusion}.
The {NN}-related literature considers free-diffusion environments on the ($\si{\micro\meter}$) scale, which include spherical reflectors and absorbers placed at arbitrary locations between a point emitter and a spherical absorbing receiver.
This is the case reported in \cite{ozdemir2021estimating,cheng2025deep} in 2D and also extended to 3D in \cite{ozdemir2024estimating}.
A less complex topology is considered in \cite{yilmaz2017machine} in a 3D environment, including a perfect spherical receiver and point and spherical transmitters.
\ac{MIMO} links in {MC} are also gaining increasing interest because of their potential to enhance transmission rates.
Examples are the $2\times 2$ \ac{MIMO} scheme in free-diffusion {MC} channels for \cite{lee2017machine,damrath2018array}, $4\times 4$ \ac{MIMO} in~\cite{ozbey2024artificial}, and asymmetric \ac{MIMO} channels comprising \num{2} emitters and \num{4} receivers in~\cite{cheng2024channel}.
These \ac{MIMO} channels lay out the geometries of point transmitters alongside spherical absorbing receivers.

Literature also evaluated testbed measurements accounting for realistic channel effects, which include the various sizes of droplets, evaporation, and unsteady flows as in~\cite{gulec2020distance}.
The testbed deploys an air compressor sprayer to release ethyl alcohol molecules and an MQ-3 alcohol sensor to detect the droplets from distances of up to $\SI{200}{\centi\meter}$.
See a summary of {MC} environments and their parameters in appendix, \Cref{tab_detection_parameters}, specifically for the entries related to channel estimation.

\noindent
\textbf{Human vessel networks}: For drug delivery applications within the human body, the model reported in \cite{mohamed2021biocyber} includes three main components.
As depicted in \Cref{fig_mohamed2021biocyber}, these components are: 1) A photo-triggered transducer interface (Gateway), which is placed at the skin surface, 2) the human vessels network as the information channel, and 3) a network of nanodevices located at the target cell.\footnote{The sketches for the bio-components in \Cref{fig_mohamed2021biocyber} do not mean to be accurate but are for concept illustration purposes only.
The figures were generated with the assistance of ChatGPT, utilizing a Da Vinci style for the drawing.
Similarly, we generated all illustrations of bio-components within this paper.}
The photo-triggered interface is attached to the skin surface and injects specific molecules into the human circulatory system.
This release mechanism is activated upon the wireless reception of a command from a remote unit, which the care providers can directly access.
The released molecules travel through the vessel segments to the target cell, where a nanonetwork is activated to release the corresponding drugs upon detecting this particular molecule.
Molecule concentration levels are evaluated using a two-compartmental model, one for the circulatory network and another for the target cell.
The evaluation of the concentration level is based on a system of two differential equations, which incorporate the parameters $k_\mathrm{in}$, $k_\mathrm{out}$, and $k_\mathrm{disp}$; refer to \cite[Eqs. (14) and (15)]{dhillon2006clinical}.
These parameters refer to the rates of molecules flowing in and out from the compartment, as represented in \Cref{fig_mohamed2021biocyber}.


