\subsubsection{{NN} Architectures to Detect Incoming Symbols}
\label{sec_NN_detectors}

Literature presents various {NN} network architectures used as detectors, primarily aimed at dynamically learning the {MC} channel parameters.
Examples include feedforward {NNs}, a general-purpose network that takes slight advantage of a priori knowledge, such as channel memory.
\ac{CNN} architectures are also reported, which are more robust to channel delays.
Moreover, the \ac{BiRNN} architecture, which is more advantageous concerning the \ac{ISI} in {MC} channels, is also discussed in the literature.
This architecture utilizes past and future samples to decode the current symbol, aiming to recover knowledge from the channel's \ac{ISI}.
Additionally, more recently, popular attention models (see \cite{vaswani2017attention}) have been incorporated into the architecture to learn dependencies in the received sequence over more extended periods.

These architectures are reported to perform detection either on a symbol-by-symbol basis or in a sequence fashion.
Symbol-by-symbol is the simplest one, where the {NN} is trained with samples corresponding to a single symbol only, like in \cite{qian2018receiver,qian2019molecular,farsad2017detection,farsad2018detection,sharma2020deep,kim2023machine}.
More robustly, in the sequence detection mode, the {NN} is trained with several symbols, where detection of the current symbol develops from observing past and future ones, like in \cite{qian2019molecular,farsad2018detection,sun2020ctbrnn}.
The architectures that follow these two approaches, as developed in the literature, are described next, and a detailed summary of their achieved performance is provided in the appendix, \Cref{tab_detection_parameters}.

\paragraph{Symbol-by-symbol detection mode}
In the symbol-by-symbol detection mode, feedforward {NNs} are employed to solve the \ac{MAP} formulation in \eqref{eq_MAP}; see \cite{farsad2017detection,farsad2018detection,sharma2020deep,shrivastava2021performance,vakilipoor2022hybrid,agrawal2022neural,bai2023temporal,koo2020deep,kim2023machine} and to find the optimal decoding threshold, as in \Cref{eq_threshold_detector}; see~\cite{qian2018receiver,qian2019molecular}.
\acp{RNN} and \acp{CNN} are also reported to solve the \ac{MAP} formulation as described in \cite{vakilipoor2022hybrid} and \cite{kara2022molecular}, respectively.
Symbol-by-symbol detectors are also trained more efficiently using distinctive features from the input sequence.
Features are evaluated based on the concentration difference of received molecules~\cite{farsad2017detection,farsad2018neural,shrivastava2021performance,vakilipoor2022hybrid,agrawal2022neural} or by taking the coefficients of a fitted polynomial curve to the incoming symbol, see \cite{vakilipoor2022hybrid}.
Other examples extract features from the received sequence using one \cite{farsad2017detection,vakilipoor2022hybrid,bai2023temporal} and three \cite{bartunik2022using,bartunik2023artificial} hidden layers \ac{CNN} architectures.

\paragraph{Sequence detection mode}
The reported {NN} architectures are more diverse in the sequence detection mode.
Reported methods not only develop feedforward {NNs} models~\cite{qian2019molecular,qian2018receiver}, but also \acp{CNN} \cite{baydas2023estimation}, \acp{RNN} encompassing \ac{LSTM} cells~\cite{farsad2017detection,baydas2023estimation}, and~\acp{BiRNN} \cite{farsad2018neural}.
Following the feedforward {NN} architecture, the authors in \cite{qian2019molecular} develop a cascade connection as threshold decoders aimed at solving the decoding problem in \eqref{eq_threshold_detector}.
As depicted in \Cref{fig_NN_cascade}, decoding the current bit ($b_k$) accounts for the previous decoded ones ($b_{k-1}$ and $b_{k-2}$).
In this way, the {NN} inherently learns the impact of \ac{ISI} in the {MC} channel. 

The long-term dependencies in the input sequence are more efficiently learned using \ac{LSTM} cells within \ac{RNN} architectures, as described in \cite{farsad2017detection,farsad2018neural}.\footnote{The major advantage of \ac{LSTM} over \ac{RNN} is avoiding the vanishing gradient problem, allowing to learn longer data dependencies; see details in appendix, \Cref{fig_NN_architecture}.}
The schematic of these decoders is depicted in \Cref{fig_LSTM_detect}, where three layers are connected in cascade, each layer is of length \num{40} and trained with \num{120}-bit sequences; see \cite[Fig. 1 b)]{farsad2017detection}.
Besides, using \ac{LSTM} networks, bidirectional architectures can be more effectively deployed to exploit the correlation of past and future samples for decoding; see \cite[Fig. 1 c)]{farsad2017detection}.
As past emissions are the source of \ac{ISI}, their knowledge can be used to cancel out their impact in the current detection.
Meanwhile, as the current emission leaks into the next ones, future samples also carry helpful information to decode the current one.
Using the available input information of past and future samples, the implemented \ac{BiRNN} model in \cite{farsad2018detection,farsad2017detection} reduces the impact of \ac{ISI} and is also reported to be more computationally efficient than the Viterbi decoder for long memory channels.

