\subsubsection{Illustrative Code Example for Symbol Detection}
\label{sec_code_decoder}

This section demonstrates the performance of the sliding \ac{BiRNN} architecture using samples from the experimental testbed illustrated in \Cref{fig_testbed_sprayer}.
The testbed sets a communication link over the distance of $\SI{1}{\meter}$ between the emitter sprayer and the receiver sensor.
Using ethanol molecules as carriers, \ac{OOK} emissions are performed with binary pulses of $T_b=\SI{4}{\second}$ duration each; see further details in \cite[Sec. II]{hofmann2022testbed}.
We use the recorded received pulse as the expected sequence for the emissions of ones, and we modeled the randomness of the received sequence with the Poisson channel model; see \cite[Eq. (87)] {jamali2019channel}.
Besides, we programmatically added noise molecules of concentration $\SI{10}{\milli\gram\per\liter}$ yielding an $\mathrm{SNR}$ of $\SI{27.5}{\decibel}$.
At the receiver, the observed sequence of pulses is sampled using the synchronization signal, which is assumed to be known.\footnote{The dataset for the sequence of pulses is accessible in \cite{hofmann2023dataset_macroscale}.}


\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \subfloat[Experimental testbed; adapted from~\cite{debus2024synchronized}.] %\textcolor{red}{Pit: Can we change the figure to "testbed\_sprayer.pdf" to avoid copyright issues, as discussed on Tuesday?}
    {\includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/testbed_sprayer.png}\label{fig_testbed_sprayer}}
    %
    \subfloat[Training loss.]{\includegraphics[width=0.34\linewidth]{figures/testbed_training.pdf}\label{fig_testbed_training}}
    %
    \subfloat[Resulting \ac{BER}.]{\includegraphics[width=0.34\linewidth]{figures/testbed_Rx_BER.pdf}\label{fig_testbed_Rx_BER}}
    %
    \caption{Experimental testbed used for {MC} and measurements, as developed in \cite{hofmann2022testbed}.}
    \label{fig_exp_testbed}
    \vspace{-.8em}
\end{figure*}


Due to channel memory, consecutive emissions result in \ac{ISI}, which hinders the use of low-complexity schemes, such as the threshold decoder.
For the decoder, we train the sliding \ac{BiRNN} scheme reported in~\cite{farsad2018sliding,farsad2018neural} as a sequence detector.
With the measured pulse duration from the experimental testbed ($\SI{28.6}{\second}$ in total), we evaluate the channel memory as $L=\lfloor\frac{\SI{28.6}{\second}}{T_b}\rfloor=7$, which also defines the number of hidden states of the \ac{BiRNN} model.\footnote{The number of hidden states in the \ac{BiRNN} corresponds with the amount of information to be retained; which in this case is not larger than the channel memory.}
For training, we use the Adam algorithm \cite{kingma2014adam} with a learning rate of \num{e-3}, along with \num{10} epochs of a batch size of \num{10}, and with the emission of \num{e5} bits.

The training loss decreases in value with increasing epochs and bit duration, as illustrated in \Cref{fig_testbed_training}.
Specifically, we observe stability after the second epoch, and losses decrease as the bit duration increases.
The resulting \ac{BER} falls within the range \numrange{e-2}{2e-6}, where the higher errors occur in the lower bit duration range of the plot ($T_b<\SI{2}{\second}$).
Besides, as the peak of received pulses lasts for around~$\SI{4}{\second}$, the resulting \ac{BER} dramatically decreases when transmissions are performed with a bit duration of $\SI{5}{\second}$ or longer, i.e., the impact of \ac{ISI} becomes negligible.


