\subsubsection{Hyperparameter Tunning}

Avoiding the manual selection of the {NN} hyperparameters, a group of techniques called hyperparameter optimization performs the automatic tuning of these; see \cite{yang2020hyperparameter}.
Yet, when applying {NNs} to {MC} problems, there are no reported methods for optimizing hyperparameters such as the number of nodes, hidden layers, or the learning rate.
Reported methods in the literature just selected these without further discussion; the impact of such hyperparameters and their optimization (see appendix, \Cref{boxTech_hyper}) remains an open topic.

\subsubsection{Channel Access in MC-based IoBNT Networks}
Channel access strategies face the problem of the unknown number of nodes in the link, which is the case in nanonetwork formation in {MC} scenarios.
Coordination of the nodes is pivotal to treating potential diseases effectively. 
Nanosensors are expected to combine their actions to simultaneously release the right amount of drugs at potential targets, such as cancer cells~\cite{mosayebi2019early}.
Protocols have been designed to develop these applications, as seen in \cite{felicetti2014tcplike}; however, the dynamics of {MC} channels in fluidic environments hinder their straightforward deployment.
{NN}-based strategies developed in computer networks for node clustering, such as those in \cite{lai2011cognitive}, can be repurposed in {MC} environments for nanosensors cluster formation as well.

