\subsection{Trends in Biocomputing}

Researchers have explored various approaches to enhance the intelligence of nanodevices and improve their computational capabilities, adhering to biocompatible premises.
Adders, multipliers, and non-linear functions are the fundamental building blocks for developing {NN} components; see appendix, \Cref{fig_NN_architecture}.
Conceiving these arithmetic operations at the nanoscale will enable the integration of the aforementioned {NN}-based transmission and reception architectures within the operational nanodevices.

Already published solutions are developed along three main directions: (i) Extend the biological role of \ac{DNA}-based chemical reactions within the cell for computing; see the evolution in \cite[Fig. 2a]{haluan2024synthetic} and \cite[Fig. 1]{nagipogu2023survey}, (ii) develop \ac{CRN} as in \cite{agiza2024phcontrolled}, and (iii) build microfluidic chips for computing as in \cite{wang2013modular}.
Towards the development of {NN} models, a major trend in the literature is the use of engineered \acp{GRN} (see \Cref{sec_biocomp_gen}) due to their programmability for complex calculations; see \cite[Sec. 4.3]{nagipogu2023survey}.
Primarily developed within the communication-engineering community, a second trend involves integrating chemical reactions within microfluidic chips, see \Cref{sec_biocomp_microfluidic}. 
Following these research streams, we trace the early developments of digital logic and \ac{DSP} operations for {NNs} in the following sections.

