\subsubsection{Digital signal processing in microfluidic chips}
\label{sec_biocomp_microfluidic}

The literature also reports models for \ac{DSP} operations in microfluidic channels, which can be also used for implementing {NNs}, see this trend within the fifth level in \cite{bi2021digital}.
A \ac{FIR} filter is modeled in \cite[Sec. V]{bicen2013systemtheoretic} to implement lowpass, stopband, and bandpass filtering of molecule concentration levels.
The impulse response (in the time domain) and transfer function (in the frequency domain) are derived for combining microfluidic straight channels of arbitrary length, turns, bifurcations, and junctions, and in drifted fluids where convection dominates diffusion.
The filter coefficients are constructed by equating the transfer function of the microfluidic channel to a desired \ac{FIR} impulse response equation.
Then, the filter coefficients are determined using the least-squares formulation, see \cite{bicen2013systemtheoretic}.\footnote{The microfluidic channel parameters can also be equated to an equivalent \ac{FIR} block using numerical results from fluid simulators, see \cite{hofmann2024openfoam}.}
In this way, a multitap microfluidic circuit performs equivalent to a \ac{FIR} filter having non-negative coefficients; see \cite[Fig. 6]{bicen2013systemtheoretic}.

The \ac{FIR} design of the microfluidic circuit can also be extended to implement the neurons of an {NN} architecture.
The linear components of a neuron can be evaluated with an \ac{FIR} structure considering its coefficients as the learnable parameters (see appendix, \Cref{fig_NN_architecture}).\footnote{Although the coefficients of the microfluidic \ac{FIR} filter are non-negative, the linear component of the neuron can be implemented after scaling the input and the coefficients.}
The non-linear component of the neuron, i.e., the activation function, can be implemented through chemical reactions integrated to the microfluidic channel.
Examples are given in \cite{bi2020chemical,walter2023real}, where a \ac{CRN} is attached to the output gate of a microfluidic channel, and similarly in \cite{amerizadeh2021bacterial} with a bacterial colony.

Notably, \ac{DSP} operations can also be implemented using graphene biofield-effect transistors integrated with microfluidic channels.
These transistors are functionalized to detect \ac{DNA} strands flowing in liquid channels (see \cite{kuscu2021fabrication, abdali2024frequencydomain}), thereby providing an interface between the molecular and electrical domains.
Once within the electric domain, additional transistors can be integrated to develop the \ac{DSP} components of {NNs} such as adders, multipliers, and non-linear functions. 

