\subsubsection{Reservoir computing in MC channels}

In pursuit of more powerful {NN} architectures, reservoir computing has also been reported in the {MC} domain to realize \ac{RNN} models~\cite{uzun2025molecular}.
Reservoir computing utilizes a static \ac{RNN} structure, placing the network's intelligent component in the output layer instead~\cite{stepney2024physical}.
The solution in \cite{uzun2025molecular} implements the reservoir using the {MC} geometry: Point transmitter-free diffusion {MC} channel-ligand spherical receptor.
This end-to-end {MC} channel implements the state update equation of the \ac{RNN} architecture; see \cite[Eq. (1)]{uzun2025molecular}.
The output layer is designed as a vector comprising samples of the number of bound molecules at the receptor, collected over a time window; see \cite[Eq. (9)]{uzun2025molecular}.
The training of this model involves adjusting the vector component as weights in the output layer, and it is implemented in a silicon interface.
The research in \cite{uzun2025molecular} also discusses the impact of {MC} channel parameters on network performance, illustrating ranges of preferred values for distances, diffusion coefficients, and binding coefficients; see 
\cite[Fig. 4]{uzun2025molecular}.

