\subsection{Illustrative Code Example and Results}
\label{sec_xai_code}

As a code example, we take the model already developed in \Cref{sec_code_dist}, which deploys a \num{2} node-{NN} as a distance estimator between immune and cancer cells.
The model first extracts two features from the number of vesicles in immune cells; these two features are the amplitude and the time coordinate (peak time) of the minimum of the slope of this sequence; see \Cref{fig_cell2cell} a) and b).
The {NN} inputs are these two features, and the output is the predicted distance, as illustrated in \Cref{fig_cell2cell}~c).
Following the work in \cite{basaran2025xai-enhanced}, we implemented the \ac{MPI} method to evaluate the significance of each feature for the {NN}'s output, and also included the \ac{SHAP} value for further illustration.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \subfloat[Permutation importance.]
    % {\includegraphics[width=0.4\linewidth]{figures/figurexailasta.pdf}\label{fig_xaia}}
    {\includegraphics[width=0.47\linewidth]{figures/figurexailasta.pdf}\label{fig_xaia}}
    \hfill
    \subfloat[Shapley summary.]{\includegraphics[width=0.47\linewidth]{figures/figurexailastd.pdf}\label{fig_xaib}}
    % %
    \caption{Feature importance analysis using \acl{MIP}.}
    \label{fig_xai}
    \vspace{-.8em}
\end{figure}

The results depicted in \Cref{fig_xai} underline the importance of the peak position.
Feature~\num{2} (peak time) demonstrates a significantly higher performance loss increase compared to Feature~\num{1} (peak amplitude) in both methods, suggesting a stronger correlation between the peak time and the distance.
Thereby, the peak time likely encodes key characteristics of vesicle-mediated communication. 
In addition to permutation-based analysis, \ac{SHAP} values provide a more nuanced view of feature contributions across individual predictions.
As illustrated in \Cref{fig_xai}~b), the $x$-axis denotes the \ac{SHAP} values, reflecting how much each feature influences the predicted distance, while the color gradient indicates the actual feature value (ranging from low in blue to high in yellow).
Notably, the peak time spans a wider \ac{SHAP} value distribution, showing it has both a stronger and more diverse influence on predictions compared to peak amplitude, whose \ac{SHAP} values are centered tightly around zero.
The consistently increasing \ac{SHAP} values for higher peak time values imply a monotonic positive relationship with distance, confirming its dominant and biologically plausible role in vesicle timing as an informative signal of cell-to-cell proximity.\footnote{We provide this code at \url{https://github.com/tkn-tub/NN_molecular_communications/tree/main/Section_V_XAI}.} 
This consistency highlights its resilience and importance in predictions.

The results in \Cref{fig_xai} are in direct correspondence with the expected properties of the {CIR} in free diffusion channels.
There exists a direct correlation between the travel time (here encoded within peak time) and the square of the distance, expressed as \( t_\mathrm{peak} \propto \frac{d^2}{D} \), as referred to in \cite[below Eq. (2.8)]{berg1993random}.
The {NN} architecture effectively filters the input associated with the peak and assesses the direct relationship between peak time and distance. 

