\subsection{Attention in the Loop with the Transformer Architecture}
\label{sec_atten}

The Transformer architecture performs exceptionally well in language translation and generation.
As the major distinction, this architecture overcomes the sequential processing of {LSTM} networks,\footnote{{LSTM} completes the processing sequentially due to the cascade connection of cells like the ones in \Cref{fig_LSTM}.} and forwards parallel capabilities by getting rid of recurrent structures as previous standard developments as in~\cite{bahdanau2014neural,wu2016googles,sutskever2014sequence,cho2014learning}.\footnote{See a perspective introduction to main core development for the Transformer architecture in the interview \cite{levy2024google}.}
Besides, the Transformer architecture integrates attention models within two feedforwards {NNs}, those for encoding and decoding\cite{vaswani2017attention}; see a reduced schematic representation in \Cref{fig_Transformer}.\footnote{We remark that a similar direction to remove recurrent structures is given in \cite{gehring2017convolutional} but using the {CNN} architecture instead.}
The attention model evaluates a context vector to support the decoder component in predicting the most likely next symbol, a concept under development in \cite{bahdanau2014neural,sukhbaatar2015endtoend,luong2015effective,zhou2016deep,gehring2017convolutional}, which has been shown to significantly enhance the alignment capabilities of neural machine translators.\footnote{Although we refer to symbols in the context of {MC} channel, the attention model in \cite{bahdanau2014neural} and the Transformer architecture in \cite{vaswani2017attention} are developed as language translators and symbols are instead referring to words.}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/Transformer.pdf}
    % \includegraphics[width=0.4\linewidth]{figures/Transformer.pdf}
    \caption{Transformers architecture (reduced schematic) \cite{vaswani2017attention}.}
    \label{fig_Transformer}
    \vspace{-.8em}
\end{figure}

The encoder and decoder in the Transformer architecture, as shown in \Cref{fig_Transformer}, implement a self-attention mechanism using the scaled dot product operation.
This architecture's second significant distinction is realized as a more efficient mechanism than the implementation with additive attention; see \cite[Sec. 4.5]{britz2017massive}.
The self-attention mechanism evaluates the product of matrices of queries, keys, and values (see \cite[Eq.~(1)]{vaswani2017attention}).
These matrices are solely based on the input sequence and a pre-multiplicative learned matrix, adjusted during training.\footnote{The self-attention mechanism differs from the recursive architecture, where these matrices are calculated based on the {LSTM} hidden states.}
Additionally, the decoder employs an attention mechanism, where the query is evaluated based on the input sequence, while the keys and values are evaluated based on the output sequence.
While the self-attention block extracts information from the input sequence, the attention block produces its output using what is relevant within the encoder's output.
Although this architecture discards {RNNs}, inherently, the attention model is a form of recurrence, as its output is used to evaluate the internal coefficients.

