\subsection{Reinforcement Learning: Smartly Actuating on the Environment}
\label{boxTech_rl}

While the traditional {ML} approaches, i.e., supervised and unsupervised learning, detect a type in the data set presented to them, Reinforcement Learning optimizes its decisions via trial-and-error interaction with an environment without the need for an a priori data set \cite{arulkumaran2017deep}.
This approach can be used to actuate the communication scheme parameters, such as the threshold for detecting incoming symbols.
{RL} in general consists of an {RL} agent and an attached environment. The environment is typically defined as a (partially observable) Markov decision process consisting of
%
\begin{itemize}
        \item a set of states $S$,
        \item a set of actions $A$,
        \item a distribution of initial states $p(s_0)$,
        \item a reward function $r : S\times A \rightarrow R$,
        \item transition probabilities $p(s_t+1 | s_t, a_t)$,
        \item termination probabilities $T(s_t,a_t)$, and
        \item a discount factor gamma element $[0,1]$ \cite{andrychowicz2020what}.
\end{itemize}
%
The agent optimizes a policy function that maps the state to a distribution over actions. Given an environment state $s_t$, the agent acts $a_t$ according to its policy function and is given a reward based on the result of the applied action. During the training, the policy function is optimized to maximize the expected future rewards \cite{andrychowicz2020what}.

The {RL} agent learns the impact of its actions, their causes, and effects, within the environment in steps, imitating the natural human learning process \cite{hoang2023drl}. The learning process itself is supported by two different functions, the policy and the value functions. 
The policy determines how the agent behaves at each new step, while the value function estimates the goodness of a state in terms of the expected reward. 
The networks used to approximate the two functions can also employ different types of {DNNs}, such as {ANNs}, {CNN}, or {RNNs}.
With the inclusion of these types of network layers, {RL} then becomes {DRL} \cite{arulkumaran2017deep}. 
The type of network used depends on the task the {RL} agent needs to solve. 


