The proof of Theorem \ref{thm:dimension}
is an induction based on contracting an edge of $T$.
In order to define a contraction of a triangulation
$\T=(T,C)$, we need some language and notation.


First, we say that $T$ contains a \emph{subdivision} if there are vertices $x,y,z$ 
of $T$ such that $xy$, $yz$, $xz$ are edges of $T$, $xyz$ is not a triangle of $T$, 
and $\{x,y,z\}\ne \bdry\T$.  

Next, an edge $e=xy$ of $T$ is \emph{contractible} if $e\not\subset\bdry T$
and the ``contraction" $T/e$ (defined below) is a simplicial complex.  Specifically
this means that there are exactly two vertices $w,z$ of 
$T$ that are adjacent to both $x$ and $y$; when this holds, we define $T/e$
to be the simplicial complex obtained from $T$ by deleting the triangles $wxy$ and $xyz$
and identifying the vertices $x$ and $y$ 
(and consequently identifying the edges $wx$ and $wy$, and the edges $xz$ and $yz$). 

We leave it to the reader to verify that if the above condition holds, then $T/e$ is indeed simplicial.
See Figure \ref{fig:contractible}.

