% Then we bring the above formula into the Wigner function definition Eq.[\ref{W_def}], refer to Appendix\ref{A.A} we can get
\begin{equation}
    \label{W_1st}
    W[\phi,\Pi;0] =\frac{1}{Z} e^{-\beta H}\;,
\end{equation}
see the {\it Supplementary Material} for more details, where $H=\int dx^3[\frac{1}{2}\Pi^2(x) + \frac{1}{2}(\nabla \phi(x))^2 + V(\phi)]$ is the classical Hamiltonian. Since both the initial distribution and the equation of motion of the Wigner function are computed neglecting $\mathcal{O}(\hbar^2)$ terms, quantum effects are retained only at order $\hbar$. This suggests that the Wigner function remains static over time when the $\mathcal{O}(\hbar^2)$ approximation is neglected. However, it should be noted that in the integral expression of Eq.~\ref{PFV with wigner2}, the integration measure evolves with time, and performing the functional integral itself is nontrivial. To implement the functional integral with interval constraints, we sample field configurations in the discrete phase space and introduce a statistical factor $\chi$, defined as:
\begin{equation}
\label{chi define}
    \chi_{FV}(\phi_{-t}^{(i,j)})=\frac{N(\phi_{-t}^{(i,j)}(x)<\phi_m)}{N}\;.
\end{equation}
Here, $(i,j)$ is to mark different sample fields, and the denominator $N$ represents the number of discrete coordinate space lattice points. In three-dimensional space, $N=N_x\cdot N_y\cdot N_z$. The numerator represents the number of grid points where the field values fall below $\phi_m$ for a given sample field $(i,j)$, and $\phi_m$ is the field value corresponding to the barrier (as shown in Fig.\ref{fig:V_phi}). From the definition formula of $\chi$, it can be seen that it is used to measure how many "quantities" of a certain field are within the target integral interval, and $\chi$ is time-dependent. Therefore, the information of time evolution here is completely contained within $\chi$. Eventually, our probability expression can be written as:
\begin{equation}
\label{Pfv_chi}
\begin{split}
    P_{FV}(t)
    % &=\frac{1}{Z}\int_{FV}\mathcal{D}\phi\frac{\mathcal{D} \Pi}{2 \pi}W(\phi,\Pi;t)\,\\
    % &=\frac{1}{Z}\int_{FV}\mathcal{D}\phi_{-t}\frac{\mathcal{D} \Pi_{-t}}{2 \pi}  W(\phi_{-t},\Pi_{-t};0)\,\\
    &=\frac{\int \mathcal{D}\phi_{-t}\frac{\mathcal{D} \Pi_{-t}}{2 \pi} \chi_{FV}(\phi_{-t})  W(\phi_{-t},\Pi_{-t};0)}{\int \mathcal{D}\phi_{-t}\frac{\mathcal{D} \Pi_{-t}}{2 \pi} W(\phi_{-t},\Pi_{-t};0)}\\
    &\approx \frac{\sum_{i j}\chi_{FV}(\phi_{-t}^{(i,j)})\cdot W(\phi_{-t}^{(i,j)},\Pi_{-t}^{(i,j)};0)}{\sum_{i j} W(\phi_{-t}^{(i,j)},\Pi_{-t}^{(i,j)};0)}\;.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Where, we can see that the $\chi$ factor comes from the classical field evolution part, while quantum effect at $\hbar$-order is contained in the Wigner function. Using the above expression, we can simulate the behavior of the decay rate at different initial temperatures.
