We simulate the time-dependent behavior of $P_{FV}$. We find that the decay processes on both sides of the critical point exhibit different characteristics: when $\beta\omega^{\star}<\beta_c\omega^{\star}$, i.e., the initial energy is greater than the potential barrier (the critical point of the initial energy), 
% and the system will decay significantly over a relatively short period of time, after that the $P_{FV}$ will increase if the temperature is large enough, this means that the wave propagating to the true vacuum region crosses the potential barrier and returns back to the false vacuum. To avoid the nonlinear effect brought by this process, our effective time range can only be taken before the first increase of $P_{FV}$, which exactly corresponds to $T_{NL}$. As the temperature drops, $\beta$ gradually approaches $\beta_c$, and we no longer observe an increase in $P_{FV}$. However, $P_{FV}$ continues to decline from the very beginning. When $P_{FV}$ drops to a certain threshold, we consider the system to have decayed, so the subsequent time is no longer meaningful. The time corresponding to this threshold is also the effective time range.
the probability of false vacuum $P_{FV}$ will have a continuous decline. When it drops to a sufficiently small threshold, we consider the system to have decayed. Therefore, the effective time range we take is before $P_{FV}$ drops to a certain threshold.
Within the valid time, we use a sliding window to fit $P_{FV}\sim \exp(-\Gamma t)$, where the window length is $25\%$ of the valid time and the goodness-of-fit threshold is $0.99$. Then, in the window that satisfies the goodness-of-fit, we identify the maximum value of $\Gamma$ in the valid windows. The reason for taking the maximum value is that we believe that the maximum $\Gamma$ here can be used as the characteristic value characterizing the decay process. 
After $\beta\omega^{\star}\gtrsim \beta_c\omega^{\star}$, the energy is less than the barrier. The system needs some time to undergo thermalization to reach a stable decay process. By observing the $P_{FV}-t$ graph, we find that the decay after thermalization behaves very stably. The decay during this period can well fit the exponential form $P_{FV}\sim exp(-\Gamma t)$. So our effective time range is set in the stable decay interval. Similarly, we are use the sliding window for fitting and extraction to obtain the final decay rate. The final result is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:gamma vs beta dif V}. In {\it Supplemental Material}, we present the scenario of $V_b=0.035$ for illustration. We obtain the variation of $\Gamma/T$ with $\beta \omega^{\star}$. And we used the formula $\ln(\Gamma/T)=\mu (\beta-\beta_0)V_b^{\frac{1}{2}}+\nu(\beta-\beta_0)^2V_b+\lambda$ to fit our results, the values we have fitted are respectively $\mu=12.29,~\nu=-54.27,~\lambda=-0.86$ and $\beta_0\omega^{\star}=0.49$, goodness of fit is $0.987$. 


