To compare our simulation results with the finite-temperature theoretical predictions from thermal equilibrium field theory, we compute the predicted decay rate by obtaining the instanton solution following the method in {\it BubbleDet}~\cite{Ekstedt:2023sqc}: $\Gamma \propto (S/2\pi T)^{1/2}e^{-S/T}\;$,
where $S$ is the Euclidean spatial action, and is given by
\begin{equation}
    \label{SB}
    S=\int dr\left[\frac{1}{2}(\frac{\partial \phi_B}{\partial r})^2+V_{eff}(\phi_B)\right]\;,
\end{equation}
where $\phi_B$ is the bounce solution, which satisfies
\begin{equation}
    \label{bounce}
    \frac{\partial^2 \phi_B}{\partial r^2}-\frac{\partial V_{eff}}{\partial\phi}=0\;.
\end{equation}
The instanton prediction is qualitatively consistent with our simulation results. However, the decay process in our simulation is inherently probabilistic and statistical, and it incorporates non-equilibrium and nonlinear effects—such as those arising from bubble dynamics during the transition ~\cite{Bian:2025twi,Pirvu:2024nbe,Pirvu:2024ova,Pirvu:2023plk}, whereas the instanton approach describes the tunneling process between two minima under thermal equilibrium conditions, the results of the two here do not exactly match.
It can be seen that the false vacuum decay rate $\Gamma$ gradually decreases as the temperature drops, see the dashed lines in Fig.~\ref{fig:gamma vs beta dif V}. When $\beta\omega^{\star}<\beta_c\omega^{\star}$, the initial energy is greater than the barrier, the decay of the system is a classical decay, the influence of temperature on the decay rate is relatively small. When $\beta\omega^{\star}>\beta_c\omega^{\star}$, $\Gamma$ will have a significant decrease. This is precisely because the initial energy is less than the barrier height, so the decay process is dominated by thermal fluctuations. When the temperature is lower, we conjecture that ensuring the reliability of our method at low temperatures may require a larger number of samples $N_{samples}$ and an extended simulation scale. In our definition and description of the decay rate, the key quantity is the time-dependent probability distribution. In {\it Supplemental Material}, we also provide diagrams of the probability distribution at the initial moment and after evolution, clearly illustrating the vacuum-decay process.


