\section{Conclusion and discussion}
In this Letter, through (1+1)-dimensional lattice classical field simulation, we evaluated the false vacuum decay rate with the Wigner function method. We observe that: 1) the false vacuum probability follows an exponential time decay form; 2) the vacuum decay rate exponentially decreases as the thermal fluctuation magnitude decrease; and 3) the decay rate decreases as the potential barrier height increases. We demonstrate that the methodology employed in this study can yield results that deviate from those obtained via the instanton method grounded in the saddle-point approximation, particularly when thermal fluctuation effects are incorporated, which are argued to originate from the incomplete thermalization of the metastable phase~\cite{Pirvu:2024nbe,Pirvu:2024ova,Hirvonen:2025hqn}. 

Our direct path integral approach circumvents limitations of saddle-point approximations, particularly in capturing non-perturbative thermal effects. This provides a framework for studying vacuum decay in scenarios when the thermal effective field perturbation theory fails. Future extensions to 3+1 dimensions could quantify gravitational wave signatures from cosmological phase transitions, leveraging the method's capability to handle inhomogeneous field configurations~\cite{Bian:2025twi}. We expect that the method developed in this Letter can be directly applied to the calculation of baryon number violation processes—specifically, the sphaleron rate~\cite{Khlebnikov:1988sr}—and may offer a promising tool for simulating baryon asymmetry generation in the early Universe.


