We averaged the power spectra of all samples and obtained their time evolution, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:spectrum evol}. The dynamics reveal distinct signatures depending on the temperature regime relative to the critical value $\beta_c$.
For systems below the critical temperature $\beta < \beta_c$, exemplified by $\beta\omega^{\star}=1.4$, the low-$k$ component of the $\phi$ power spectrum exhibits a rapid initial rise, followed by a gradual decay approaching a quasi-stationary value. This corresponds to thermal activation over the barrier and subsequent relaxation into the true vacuum basin. The power spectrum of $\Pi$ shows similar behavior to that of $\phi$ and maintains nearly constant amplitude fluctuations across all wavenumbers, reflecting persistent thermal noise from residual field excitations.
