\begin{abstract}
This paper advances a radical restructuring of scholarly validation by reframing peer review as an open, iterative system of identity-linked public commentary. It challenges the epistemic assumptions of the current gatekeeping model, which privileges opacity, anonymity, and institutional inertia, and instead introduces a framework grounded in transparency, attribution, and ongoing engagement. Through the integration of scalable blockchain infrastructure for immutable citation and commentary logs, and AI systems capable of cross-disciplinary synthesis, contradiction detection, and valuation mapping, the model reconceives scholarly knowledge as a persistent and dialogical structure. This shift facilitates not only faster dissemination and real-time review, but also epistemic continuity, the integration of null results, and the rehabilitation of retractions as critical contributions. By structuring intellectual labour as traceable conversation rather than credentialed assertion, the framework aligns scientific communication with its underlying philosophical mandate: the public evolution of truth.

\textbf{Keywords:} scholarly communication, peer review, epistemology, blockchain, AI synthesis, citation networks, reproducibility, identity verification, null results, open science
\end{abstract}




