\section{Introduction}

The structure of contemporary scholarly communication is in crisis. What was once designed as a system for validating knowledge through peer engagement has ossified into a mechanism of exclusion, delay, and epistemic inertia. Traditional peer review, often anonymous and unaccountable, functions less as a means of scholarly dialogue than as an instrument of gatekeeping. The effects are measurable: valuable research is delayed or suppressed, replication studies are disregarded, and negative or null results are systematically ignored. This is not merely an inefficiency—it is a distortion of epistemic progress.

Knowledge production does not end at publication. Every paper, dataset, and model must be subjected to continuous scrutiny, contextualisation, and refinement. Yet the current system treats publication as a terminus rather than a node in a broader intellectual network. Reviewers remain invisible, their judgments undocumented. The consequence is a brittle scholarly archive—one that cannot be interrogated, commented upon, or adaptively revised in light of emerging insight.

This framework proposes a radical restructuring: peer review as structured public commentary, publication as the beginning of scholarly integration, and all contributions—affirmative or critical—linked to persistent identity and recorded on immutable, append-only systems. By reconceiving validation as a transparent, federated, and accountable process, this model foregrounds contribution, not credential; dialogue, not doctrine; and epistemic traceability, not anonymous authority. In doing so, it charts a path from a closed, static archive to a living, dynamic intellectual ecosystem.
\subsection{The Crisis of Scholarly Validation}

The current scholarly validation system, dominated by pre-publication peer review, rests on an anachronistic premise: that a small, anonymised set of individuals can unilaterally determine the worth, accuracy, and relevance of a piece of work behind closed doors. This assumption is neither epistemically defensible nor practically scalable. It fosters a culture of gatekeeping, delays publication by months or years, and excludes dissenting or unorthodox perspectives that might otherwise advance understanding. The consequences are systemic—entire research programmes are shaped not by iterative knowledge but by the biases and risk aversion of hidden arbiters.

Peer review in its traditional form fails to meet the standards of transparency and replicability expected of the scientific method itself. Decisions are opaque, reviewers unaccountable, and critiques unrecorded. Worse, the mechanism structurally suppresses negative results and null findings, reinforcing a publication bias that distorts the evidential landscape. The literature thus becomes a curated fiction of success, devoid of the necessary corrections and contradictions that fuel actual progress.

This validation crisis is not merely procedural—it is epistemological. When the mechanisms for evaluating truth claims are hidden, unreplicable, and inconsistent, they cannot support a credible framework for knowledge. To sustain scholarly legitimacy, the validation process must be visible, persistent, and challengeable. It must treat review not as a private judgment, but as a scholarly act recorded, attributed, and integrated into the evolving corpus of inquiry.
\subsection{From Gatekeeping to Commentary}

The prevailing model of academic gatekeeping is predicated on the false ideal of epistemic finality. Manuscripts are judged as either worthy or unworthy of entrance into the archive, an archive treated as static, canonical, and closed. This binary model reduces review to arbitration and forecloses ongoing dialogue. It displaces the process of understanding with institutional ritual, where acceptance functions as an imprimatur of truth rather than an invitation to scrutiny.

In contrast, commentary-based validation reorients peer interaction as a continuous and public engagement with claims. Rather than pass or fail decisions made in secret, reviews become persistent, attributable interventions into the text—annotative, comparative, corrective. This transforms each publication into a node in a network of dialogue, not a solitary monument of unchallenged authority. Scholarship thus shifts from the model of closure to one of evolution.

Gatekeeping obscures the mechanics of dissent; commentary embeds them visibly. Each annotation or response carries the trace of its author, a temporal mark, and a subject position. Knowledge is no longer filtered by invisible hands, but elaborated upon in view of all. This structure decentralises epistemic authority and aligns the process of scholarship with its purpose: not the performance of legitimacy, but the production, interrogation, and refinement of understanding.
\subsection{Aims of the Framework}

This framework seeks to re-engineer scholarly communication by treating knowledge production as an iterative, publicly visible process anchored in identity and traceability. Its aim is not to merely replace existing mechanisms, but to reconstitute the epistemic assumptions that underlie them. Validation is not the act of sealing knowledge in authoritative form, but of exposing it to structured, continuous, and accountable interrogation.

The framework promotes the replacement of closed peer review with identity-linked commentary, each contribution recorded immutably and openly. In doing so, it enables not only greater transparency but also the construction of intellectual credit systems that account for review, correction, and synthesis—not merely initial publication. It establishes a regime in which retractions, null results, and interpretive divergence are seen as integral to progress, not blemishes to be hidden.

By aligning technological infrastructure with philosophical clarity, this system reframes the scholarly archive as a living corpus. It invites AI agents to assist in mapping, summarising, and triangulating contributions, ensuring that every piece of scholarship remains embedded in a dense mesh of reference, critique, and evolution. The goal is not perfection, but integrity: a structure that reflects knowledge as conversation, traceable and contestable, rather than credential as a proxy for truth.





\section{Limitations of the Current Peer Review System}

The prevailing architecture of peer review—conceived as a pre-publication gatekeeping mechanism—is epistemologically and structurally inadequate for the demands of contemporary knowledge production. It embodies a model of intellectual validation whose foundations are historically contingent, institutionally constrained, and methodologically opaque. Built on assumptions of authority centralisation and anonymised critique, the current system prioritises editorial confidence over reproducibility, insularity over intersubjective testing, and credentialed proximity over open review. The consequences are multifold: structural delay, hermetic epistemologies, and suppressed feedback loops, all of which hinder the accumulation, correction, and legitimate distribution of knowledge.

At its core, the anonymous nature of conventional peer review severs accountability from evaluation. While intended to reduce bias, anonymity systematically undermines the epistemic integrity of commentary. Without persistent identity, reviewers are not embedded within the reputational consequences of their evaluations. This lack of traceability fosters an incentive environment that tolerates negligence, unsubstantiated rejection, and in some cases, coordinated obstructionism against ideas that challenge prevailing disciplinary orthodoxy \citep{smith2006peer}. From an epistemological perspective, knowledge gains traction through critical transparency—by making the source and content of criticism available for evaluation itself. An anonymous review cannot be interrogated, cited, or contested effectively in a cumulative epistemic process.

The latency imposed by editorial pipelines introduces a temporal bottleneck that is incompatible with both the velocity of current discovery and the iterative refinement demanded by methodologically intensive research. In rapidly evolving fields—e.g., genomics, AI safety, or epidemiology—where models and data are revised weekly, a publication delay of 6--12 months produces not only epistemic obsolescence but also a distortion of incentives: scholars optimise for acceptance over immediacy, suppressing negative results or divergent lines of inquiry in favour of consensus-conforming narratives more likely to clear the review hurdle \citep{sever2020out}. These artefacts are not incidental but systemic: the editorial model is path-dependent, evolved for a paper-based era, and now ill-suited to digital systems of knowledge exchange.

Furthermore, the current system fragments knowledge into discipline-bound silos, conferring legitimacy not by methodological soundness or replicability but by disciplinary conformity. Journals function as epistemic fiefdoms, enforcing lexicons, citation protocols, and argumentative structures that inhibit cross-disciplinary translation. This results in a static knowledge base: ideas are not contested across paradigms, data is not reused fluidly, and methods remain ghettoised. Interdisciplinary synthesis is rare not because it is infeasible but because the structures of review disincentivise it \citep{rafols2012diversity}.

The absence of a persistent, digitally verifiable identity schema exacerbates these problems. In a distributed commentary system, identity must be cryptographically and reputationally anchored. Authors, reviewers, and commentators require persistent public keys, linked to institutional credentials, historical review records, and co-citation networks. This identity architecture enables fine-grained weighting of reviews, the construction of reputational indices, and the emergence of reviewer historiography as an object of study. It also deters sabotage and trolling—since critique is no longer ephemeral but integrated into one’s scholarly legacy. Trust, in this model, is not presumed by affiliation but earned through traceable engagement over time. It is cumulative, not conferred.

In mathematical terms, the existing peer review system can be viewed as an epistemic filter function $F: P \rightarrow \{0,1\}$ where $P$ is the space of all proposed papers and $F(p) = 1$ indicates acceptance. However, this binary filtration fails to capture the conditional quality of knowledge: that some findings are provisional, some replicable with error bounds, some contested but valuable. A more rigorous model is a vector-valued evaluation $E: P \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^n$, where each component measures a dimension of scholarly value—e.g., replicability, originality, methodological soundness, cross-disciplinary uptake. Such a function cannot be constructed from three anonymous reviews. It requires a distributed network of expert engagement with persistent identities and longitudinal traceability.

The limitations of the existing model are not flaws of execution but of design. They reflect a misalignment between the mechanisms of validation and the structure of knowledge itself—a structure that is fluid, contestable, and irreducible to static binary judgments. The transition to a system of open, identity-anchored commentary is not merely an administrative improvement. It is a foundational epistemological shift: from knowledge as credentialed assertion to knowledge as an evolving, contested, and traceable object.

\subsection{Anonymity and Lack of Accountability}

Let us define the knowledge validation process as a mapping $V : (P, R) \rightarrow \mathbb{B}$ where $P$ denotes a proposed paper, $R$ a set of reviewers, and $\mathbb{B} = \{0, 1\}$ the binary space representing accept or reject decisions. In the standard peer review model, each $r_i \in R$ contributes a privately communicated assessment $a_i : P \rightarrow \mathbb{B}$, where reviewer identity is anonymised and $V$ is implemented by editorial aggregation of $\{a_1, \ldots, a_n\}$. This mechanism design lacks observability, traceability, and feedback stability. It is epistemically and mathematically degenerate under adversarial conditions, as there is no function $T : R \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^k$ such that the reliability, expertise, or bias of a reviewer can be quantitatively incorporated into $V$ ex post.

Epistemologically, peer review under anonymity fractures the dialectical foundation of knowledge, which according to Popperian and Habermasian frameworks requires that criticism be public, subject to reply, and grounded in intersubjective communicability \citep{habermas1984theory, popper2002logic}. Anonymous judgement escapes dialectical engagement and instead instantiates a form of procedural epistemic closure. In formal terms, let $C : P \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^n$ be the commentary trace associated with a paper $P$. Under anonymised review, $\mathrm{dim}(\mathrm{dom}(C)) \ll n$, reducing the effective information available to future readers, replicators, and synthesising agents.

The anonymity of peer reviewers facilitates a breakdown of Bayesian updating in the academic community. If a critique $\phi$ is issued against a claim $\psi$ but lacks identifiable provenance, then the epistemic weight $w(\phi)$ cannot be updated through reinforcement or contradiction across independent engagements. The trust model degenerates into a flat prior. In contrast, if $\phi$ is issued by agent $\alpha$ with persistent identity and historical trace $H_\alpha$, then future observers may set $w(\phi) \propto f(H_\alpha)$, where $f$ is a learned trust function computed over $\alpha$’s review performance, citation relevance, and error detection rate. In an AI-augmented system, such a trust function $f$ can be updated continuously by supervised learning over user feedback and subsequent outcome correlation, transforming review quality into a computable feature \citep{zhang2021review}.

Trolls and saboteurs are a corollary of anonymity. Without consequence, misaligned agents can insert noise into the epistemic stream. Let the probability of malicious review activity be $p_m$, and let $q$ be the expected informational degradation per such review. Then the expected epistemic loss under an anonymous regime with $n$ reviewers is $L = n \cdot p_m \cdot q$, unmitigated by any weighting or correction mechanism. Introducing persistent identity transforms $p_m$ into a time-decreasing function, $p_m(t)$, due to the feedback loops of reputational decrement and peer sanctioning.

Accountability can be instantiated through digital identity primitives. Let each agent $\alpha$ in the system possess a public key $k_\alpha$ and identity assertion $\iota_\alpha$ backed by institutional or governmental verification. Then every review, correction, or commentary $c_i$ is published as $\mathrm{sig}_{k_\alpha}(c_i)$, linked immutably to $\alpha$. The review graph $G = (V, E)$, where vertices $V$ are agents and edges $E$ are commentary links, becomes a weighted, directed reputation network. This graph can be mined by AI agents for influence mapping, reviewer alignment, and epistemic lineage tracking \citep{hoffmann2020authorship}.

The consequence of eliminating anonymity is a paradigm of cumulative, contestable, and visible critique. It transforms review from a gatekeeping act to a communicative gesture. Reviews are no longer terminal verdicts but initial positions in an open, addressable space. Trust becomes a function of exposure to critique, response quality, and iterative engagement. Knowledge grows not under cover, but in confrontation.

\subsection{Delayed Dissemination and Institutional Bottlenecks}

Let us denote the knowledge production timeline as a discrete sequence $\{T_0, T_1, \ldots, T_n\}$ where each $T_i$ represents a milestone in the lifecycle of a scientific contribution: conception ($T_0$), data collection ($T_1$), analysis ($T_2$), manuscript drafting ($T_3$), peer submission ($T_4$), review cycles ($T_5, \ldots, T_k$), and publication ($T_n$). The latency $\Delta = T_n - T_0$ is often on the order of 6--24 months, with $\Delta_r = T_n - T_4$ representing the institutional delay attributable solely to journal processing and peer review. In high-impact venues, $\Delta_r$ can exceed one year \citep{perez2021reviewtime}. This temporal inefficiency constitutes not merely a logistical issue but an epistemic degradation, particularly in fast-moving disciplines where the half-life of experimental relevance is shorter than $\Delta_r$.

The delay acts as an informational bottleneck, reducing the effective velocity of the research network. Let $N(t)$ represent the set of all available findings at time $t$ and let $U(t)$ be the subset of $N(t)$ that is accessible through publication channels. Under institutional bottlenecks, we have $|U(t)| \ll |N(t)|$, creating a state of knowledge opacity. This impacts not only the rate of scientific progress but the replicability ecosystem. Early-access data that could inform cross-validation studies is sequestered in private pipelines. This delays downstream validation, inhibits error detection, and prevents independent teams from deriving orthogonal insights \citep{gans2017science}.

Institutional publication systems reinforce this latency structurally. Editorial triage, multiple rounds of blind review, formatting constraints, and embargoes are not merely antiquated artefacts—they are intentional scarcity devices. Journals have evolved as prestige allocation systems rather than optimal dissemination mechanisms \citep{lariviere2015oligopoly}. The rent-seeking behaviour of major publishers manifests in both closed access models and protracted review windows, which ensure the exclusivity of information flow and the entrenchment of editorial gatekeeping. These bottlenecks are not epistemologically defensible; they are economically incentivised asymmetries.

Furthermore, the reliance on fixed, journal-based dissemination imposes discrete epistemic timeframes on what is inherently a continuous process. Let $K(t)$ represent the cumulative corpus of human knowledge at time $t$. Traditional publication introduces quantised surges into $K(t)$ based on release dates, rather than enabling a continuous update stream. This violates the informational smoothness requirement for high-resolution machine synthesis. AI agents trained on academic corpora are disproportionately influenced by release cadences rather than research value, biasing output generation, summary weighting, and knowledge graph interpolation \citep{beltagy2019scibert}.

To overcome these barriers, dissemination must be reframed as an open, version-controlled stream. Using blockchain or append-only ledgers, each research artefact—data, methods, drafts, code, commentary—can be registered with a persistent hash and timestamp. The result is a continuous knowledge vector $\vec{K}(t)$ where each contribution $\kappa_i$ is indexed by time, author, and semantic domain. AI agents can then perform continuous validation, semantic linking, and trust scoring across $\vec{K}(t)$ without waiting for editorial release. Moreover, the latency $\Delta_r$ collapses to a computational minimum: $\Delta_r \approx \delta_t$, where $\delta_t$ is the propagation delay across the distributed network.

Crucially, real-time dissemination also enables epistemic antifragility. When findings are made immediately accessible and open to structured commentary, the system absorbs criticism as signal, not noise. Feedback loops are accelerated, and error correction becomes endogenous to the process rather than dependent on rare post-publication notices. In such a system, the value of a contribution is not its date of final journal appearance but the velocity and volume of its critical engagement—metrics that can be dynamically computed and verified \citep{mcgillivray2020speed}.

Hence, institutional bottlenecks are not neutral delays; they constitute epistemic distortion fields. They obstruct feedback, suppress correction, and decouple the actual act of discovery from its social integration. To eliminate these bottlenecks is not to undermine review but to relocate it—from a precondition of dissemination to a continuous property of knowledge existence.


\subsection{Disciplinary Siloing and Static Scholarship}

Let the corpus of global scholarly knowledge be defined as a multidimensional vector space $\mathcal{K} = \bigcup_{d=1}^{D} \mathcal{K}_d$, where each subspace $\mathcal{K}_d$ corresponds to a disciplinary domain indexed over $d \in \{1, \ldots, D\}$. In the present system, knowledge production is functionally restricted to operations within $\mathcal{K}_d$ for each $d$, with limited projection or translation between distinct subspaces. This architectural partitioning, enforced by journal boundaries, reviewer familiarity constraints, and epistemic formatting norms, results in low inter-domain tensor alignment. That is, the inner product $\langle \mathcal{K}_i, \mathcal{K}_j \rangle$ for $i \neq j$ asymptotically approaches zero, rendering cross-disciplinary synthesis sparse and noisy.

Epistemically, this siloing constrains what Kuhn termed the “structure of scientific revolutions” \citep{kuhn1970structure}. Paradigm shifts require the ability to juxtapose anomalous data from one discipline against conceptual schemas from another. However, when peer review mechanisms penalise terminological deviance or reject heuristic analogies not grounded in domain orthodoxy, the evolutionary epistemology of science is arrested. Let $\theta$ represent a novel hypothesis and $\Pi_d$ the disciplinary projection operator. The probability of $\theta$ surviving review within $\mathcal{K}_d$ is given by $P(\theta \mid \Pi_d)$, which under siloed structures is conditional on $\theta$ conforming to $\mathcal{L}_d$, the lexicon and methodological canon of that domain. When $\theta \notin \mathcal{L}_d$, $P(\theta \mid \Pi_d) \rightarrow 0$ despite possible cross-domain explanatory power.

The result is a systemic suppression of recombinant knowledge. Studies in bibliometric topology have shown that citations tend to cluster along intra-disciplinary edges, producing high modularity graphs with weak inter-community bridges \citep{leydesdorff2011interdisciplinarity}. This limits the emergence of innovation, which is often correlated with structural holes in citation networks \citep{uzzi2013atypical}. A static epistemic structure is one in which the time derivative of methodological cross-pollination $\frac{dM_{ij}}{dt} = 0$, where $M_{ij}$ is the matrix encoding method usage across domains $i$ and $j$. Without dynamic exchange, fields become autocorrelated, performing local optimisation in stagnating epistemic basins.

Artificial intelligence has the potential to mitigate this fragmentation by embedding knowledge objects in high-dimensional semantic manifolds. Consider the embedding function $\phi: \mathcal{K} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^n$, learned through transformer-based architectures on large-scale scholarly corpora \citep{cachan2023crossfield}. When review commentary, method descriptions, and data summaries are embedded alongside traditional papers, AI agents can identify latent semantic isomorphisms across domains—even when surface terminology diverges. However, the closed nature of disciplinary publication and the embargo of review data restrict training sets, forcing AI models to overfit to canonical discourse and underperform on interdisciplinary analogues.

Moreover, disciplinary siloing restricts dynamic reinterpretation. In an ideal system, knowledge artefacts are not immutable but recontextualisable. Let $A$ be an artefact (paper, dataset, theorem) and $C_d(t)$ the commentary trace from domain $d$ at time $t$. Traditional systems instantiate $C_d(t)$ as sparse, editorially filtered, and temporally bounded. A decentralised and identity-linked commentary model allows $C_d(t)$ to be continuous, addressable, and semantically versioned. The reactivation of dormant results—what Merton called “obliteration by incorporation” \citep{merton1968matthew}—can then be computationally reversed: artefacts long submerged in disciplinary archives can resurface as AI detects contextual salience under new paradigms.

Ultimately, the disciplinary silo is not simply a boundary of convenience—it is a hard constraint on the topology of knowledge evolution. It limits the epistemic entropy of the system, preventing the stochastic recombination that drives discovery. A dynamic scholarship ecosystem requires continuous cross-domain projection, real-time method mapping, and persistent commentary trails that transcend journal walls. In formal terms, the epistemic acceleration $\alpha_e = \frac{d^2K}{dt^2}$ is positively correlated with the degree of trans-domain reusability of methods, data, and critique. Breaking silos is not a gesture toward openness. It is a mathematical precondition for epistemic growth.







\section{A New Model: Peer Review as Structured Public Commentary}

Let us redefine the act of peer review not as a gatekeeping binary but as a temporally indexed, identity-linked function of distributed commentary. Consider a research artefact $A \in \mathcal{K}$, where $\mathcal{K}$ is the corpus of extant scholarly knowledge. Under the traditional model, a static evaluation function $f: A \rightarrow \{0,1\}$ is executed by a closed set of reviewers $R = \{r_1, \dots, r_n\}$, typically under anonymity and editorial control. This yields a decision tuple $(f(A), \text{comments})$ which is neither reproducible nor queryable as an epistemic object.

In contrast, we propose a model where the review function becomes $C: A \times T \rightarrow \mathcal{E}$, mapping the artefact and time to a space of open, signed, and append-only commentaries $\mathcal{E}$, each identifiable by digital signature and linked reputation weight. Each commentary $e \in \mathcal{E}$ is defined as a tuple $e = (k_\alpha, \tau, \phi)$, where $k_\alpha$ is the public key of the reviewer $\alpha$, $\tau$ is the timestamp, and $\phi$ is the structured evaluation payload—comprising assertions, criticisms, endorsements, and linkages. These evaluations accumulate into a commentary graph $G = (V, E)$ where $V$ are identities and $E$ are directed, typed references between artefacts and reviewers. The epistemic weight of $A$ at time $t$ becomes a computable property over $G_t$.

This model allows epistemic value to be restructured along continuous, plural, and reversible dimensions. Each review becomes a node in a knowledge topology, not a sealed verdict. AI agents, operating on the graph $G$, can compute time-decayed trust scores, identify emergent consensus, flag contradictory threads, and propose meta-reviews. The commentary stream for $A$ is not filtered for editorial cohesion but harvested as raw epistemic material. The interpretative power is shifted from editors to analytic systems and scholarly communities \citep{gillies2020semantic}.

The implications are profound. In such a model, publication ceases to be an endpoint. A paper becomes a stable anchor for a vector field of interpretative motion. Corrections, clarifications, rebuttals, replications, and even derivative methods are all linked semantically and temporally. Knowledge is no longer an output; it becomes an attractor in a dynamical system of thought.

Critically, this model mandates persistent identity. Review without identification is mere opinion; review with accountable identity is cumulative knowledge. The reputation of each agent $\alpha$ is a function $\rho_\alpha(t)$, continuously updated based on the utility, accuracy, and uptake of their contributions. We may define $\rho_\alpha(t) = f(h_\alpha(t), \delta_\alpha(t))$ where $h_\alpha(t)$ is the history of signed evaluations and $\delta_\alpha(t)$ is the discrepancy measure between predicted and validated claims. AI systems trained on this data can dynamically adjust visibility, weighting, and recommendation flows for each agent’s commentary, thus incentivising clarity, rigour, and humility \citep{wang2020review}.

A key element is the transformation of review into structured data. Rather than unstructured text, each $\phi$ is a JSON-LD or RDF graph capturing claims, methods, cited dependencies, confidence intervals, critique types, and ontology tags. This structure enables AI agents to perform transitive closure on epistemic links, model claim propagation, and track conceptual lineage across disciplines. The review process thus becomes a process of \textit{encoding} thought—ensuring that each intellectual move is both human-readable and machine-parseable \citep{li2022knowledge}.

Moreover, this commentary model enhances testability. With identity, timestamps, and structured payloads, every claim can be matched to replications, contradicting results, or reuse in derivative studies. Reviews predicting future findings are marked as such; those falsified by later work are downgraded. The epistemic quality of a claim becomes probabilistic and temporally variant, not binary and fixed.

Hence, the new model rejects the unscalable fiction of universal judgement. It replaces it with a distributed computation of scholarly value—grounded in open commentary, continuous revision, and traceable identity. Knowledge is validated not by fiat but by the visible labour of scrutiny, reuse, contradiction, and response.




\subsection{Open Review Linked to Immutable Records}

To reconstruct peer review as a transparent and accountable epistemic process, each scholarly commentary must be rendered traceable, verifiable, and permanently accessible. The use of blockchain technology—specifically the original unbroken Bitcoin protocol as implemented in Bitcoin SV (BSV)—provides the necessary cryptographic infrastructure to achieve this. Each review, critique, or revision may be treated as a distinct epistemic artefact, with a verifiable hash representing its contents stored immutably on-chain, and the full content distributed through decentralised storage systems.

Let $A$ be a scholarly artefact (e.g., a paper, dataset, or method description). We define the hash of this artefact as $h_A = H(A)$, where $H$ is a cryptographic hash function such as SHA-256. This hash $h_A$ is published to the BSV blockchain in an OP\_RETURN script or structured smart unlocking script, forming the base transaction $\mathcal{T}_A$. Peer commentaries, denoted as $\phi_i$, are also hashed and anchored as child transactions $\mathcal{T}_{\phi_i}$, linked explicitly to $\mathcal{T}_A$ via transaction ancestry or metadata fields. This produces an append-only commentary graph $\mathcal{G}_A = (\{\mathcal{T}_A, \mathcal{T}_{\phi_1}, \ldots, \mathcal{T}_{\phi_n}\}, \mathcal{E})$, where $\mathcal{E}$ encodes semantic and temporal dependencies.

Unlike Ethereum-based systems which impose significant gas costs and rely on Turing-complete contract systems that evolve over time, BSV adheres to a fixed protocol rule set. This immutability in design is central to scientific provenance. Protocol-level stability ensures that records created today remain verifiable under the same validation rules decades hence, aligning with the archival demands of academic recordkeeping. The capacity of BSV to scale with gigabyte- and terabyte-sized blocks ensures that even high-volume review ecosystems—spanning raw data, model weights, and structured reviewer commentary—can be handled directly on-chain or via anchored pointers.

The system operates in two layers: (1) a hash-indexed ledger on-chain that guarantees the immutability of all assertions and their chronological order; and (2) a content-addressed distributed layer, such as IPFS or BitFS, that stores the full artefacts. Each review $\phi_i$ must be signed by the reviewer's identity key $k_{\alpha_i}$, such that $\mathrm{sig}_{k_{\alpha_i}}(\phi_i)$ can be validated independently of the storage or retrieval layer. These digital signatures provide non-repudiation, enabling the computation of reviewer reputation over time based on uptake, alignment with replicable findings, or subsequent correction. The key property is \textit{immutability with identity}—no review can be deleted, obfuscated, or posted anonymously without consequence.

This architecture satisfies all core properties of an audit-capable scholarly review system:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
  \item \textbf{Immutability}: No review or publication record can be retroactively changed.
  \item \textbf{Traceability}: Each commentary is timestamped and linked to a reviewer’s persistent identity.
  \item \textbf{Fork-resistance}: Competing interpretations or reviews form visible branches in $\mathcal{G}_A$, not hidden forks.
  \item \textbf{Scalability}: Reviews, datasets, and updates can be submitted continuously with minimal overhead.
  \item \textbf{Composability}: Structured commentary can be parsed and linked semantically for AI-based validation and aggregation.
\end{enumerate}

The practical implications are significant. A reviewer identifying a methodological flaw no longer submits a private letter to an editor, but instead publishes a signed, timestamped note with structured metadata linked to the original artefact. AI agents indexing the review graph can trace all commentary on $A$, detect contradictory claims, measure the weight of evidence in favour or against particular findings, and compute epistemic trust metrics across reviewers. The academic record becomes not a patchwork of PDFs, but a living and machine-parsable discourse network.

While formal academic studies specifically on BSV’s application in this area are emerging, existing literature on the use of blockchain for timestamping and provenance supports this architectural model. For example, \citet{sun2021blockchain} explore blockchain-based infrastructures for scholarly communication, and \citet{yli2016current} provide an overview of blockchain systems applied to digital records. Although these do not specifically cover BSV, their validation of the theoretical underpinnings affirms the feasibility of this model when scaled on protocols that do not impose arbitrary throughput or scripting limitations.

\begin{quote}
\textit{“The blockchain's properties of immutability and decentralised trust offer compelling advantages for establishing provenance, accountability, and auditability in scientific communications.”} \citep{sun2021blockchain}
\end{quote}

In sum, the integration of structured open review with an immutable blockchain-based ledger reconstitutes the epistemic authority of peer review through transparency, identity, and permanence. It replaces unverifiable process with verifiable structure—and offers a substrate where knowledge may be evaluated not by editorial closure but by persistent, accountable engagement.



\subsection{Commentary as an Iterative Scholarly Trace}

In a truly functional knowledge system, peer commentary must be reconceptualised as an iterative and cumulative trace, not as an isolated artefact of initial publication. Let $A$ be a scholarly object and let $\mathcal{C}_A = \{\phi_1, \phi_2, \ldots, \phi_n\}$ be the ordered set of commentaries upon $A$ made by peers at various times $t_i$, with $\phi_i = f(k_{\alpha_i}, A, t_i)$ denoting a signed assertion by identity key $k_{\alpha_i}$. The process $\mathcal{C}_A$ forms a temporally ordered Markov chain under the assumption that the $i$-th commentary may depend upon the artefact and the preceding commentaries, but not on future states. This trace evolves epistemic understanding in a non-reversible, cumulative process.

In epistemological terms, each commentary is not merely evaluative but constitutive. That is, the scholarly identity of $A$ is partially defined by its commentary trace $\mathcal{C}_A(t)$. As per Hacking’s dynamic constructivist view \citep{hacking1999social}, scientific facts are not discovered in vacuo but shaped and stabilised through networks of criticism, revision, and contextual reinterpretation. Mathematically, we may view this as a mapping from $A$ to an evolving object $A^{\prime}(t)$ where:
\[
A^{\prime}(t) = A + \sum_{i=1}^{n_t} w_i \cdot \phi_i,
\]
with each $w_i$ a weight assigned to $\phi_i$ based on credibility, traceability, and downstream influence. This weighted commentary sum forms a differential epistemic object—dynamic, revisable, and historically indexed.

Such traces must be both persistent and machine-parseable. In traditional scholarly publishing, commentaries are sequestered as letters to the editor, post-publication blog posts, or informal conversations. None of these contribute to a structured commentary chain. In the iterative model, every evaluative action—critique, extension, replication, contradiction—is indexed, hashed, and stored as an append-only extension of $\mathcal{C}_A$. Each $\phi_i$ is structured as a typed object: $\phi_i = (\tau_i, k_{\alpha_i}, \theta_i, \mu_i)$, where $\tau_i$ is the timestamp, $\theta_i$ the logical claim or interpretative assertion, and $\mu_i$ the modality (e.g., criticism, endorsement, reinterpretation, derivation). This makes $\mathcal{C}_A$ a formally enumerable, queryable trace graph rather than a textual afterthought.

AI agents can exploit this structure to model knowledge evolution. Using graph neural networks or attention-based memory over $\mathcal{C}_A$, agents may detect emergent consensus clusters, epistemic forks, or commentary saturation points. A commentary $\phi_j$ that receives multiple downstream references, semantic support, or integration into derivative works can be weighted more heavily. Conversely, those flagged as contradictory, corrected, or refuted decay in weight. This commentary-tracing function becomes the real arbiter of scholarly authority—measurable, distributed, and temporally sensitive.

Replications, in this model, are not binary validations but gradient contributions to $\mathcal{C}_A$. A successful replication adds a vector $\phi_r$ with high credibility weight and empirical backing. A failed replication adds a negative weight. The epistemic state of $A$ is thus no longer determined by its initial publication status, but by the vector field defined by $\mathcal{C}_A$. This model aligns with recent literature advocating for dynamic evaluation ecosystems in science \citep{nosek2015promoting} and for layering interpretative networks over published artefacts \citep{bernal2022rethinking}.

Further, commentary should itself be recursively commentable. If $\phi_i$ contains a methodological claim or critique, subsequent scholars may issue meta-commentary $\phi_{i,j}$, building a hierarchy of evaluations over time. This recursive trace forms a higher-order epistemic lattice, $\mathcal{C}_A^{\ast}$, wherein each node represents not just primary commentaries but their derivatives. This mirrors citation network stratification but in real time and with full interpretative structure.

Ultimately, the iterative commentary trace transforms knowledge validation from a sealed ritual into a continuous, computational process. The epistemic value of a paper, model, or dataset is not its initial review score, but the depth, coherence, and velocity of its evolving trace. Knowledge becomes not a pointwise object, but a path.


\subsection{From Acceptance to Integration: Publication as the Start}

In the conventional model, publication serves as the terminal act in the epistemic lifecycle of a research artefact. Let $A$ be a scholarly work submitted to a peer-reviewed venue, undergoing a decision function $D(A) \in \{0, 1\}$ representing rejection or acceptance. Upon acceptance ($D(A) = 1$), the paper is published and enters the archival record. This model presupposes a discrete validation logic: once accepted, the paper is canonised; once rejected, it is excluded from formal discourse. Such binarisation is both epistemologically naïve and computationally brittle.

In contrast, a robust knowledge system treats publication as an initial condition, not a final state. The act of making $A$ public does not determine its value—it exposes it to scrutiny, reuse, contradiction, and cumulative synthesis. Formally, let $t_0$ be the time of publication and let $\mathcal{C}_A(t)$ be the commentary trace defined over $t > t_0$. The knowledge weight of $A$, denoted $W_A(t)$, is a time-dependent function:
\[
W_A(t) = \int_{t_0}^{t} f(\phi(\tau), r(\tau), \rho(\tau)) \, d\tau,
\]
where $\phi$ are peer commentaries, $r$ are replication signals, and $\rho$ are reuse metrics. Thus, publication merely initiates the integral—it is the starting point of epistemic accretion.

This reconceptualisation aligns with a shift from evaluation to \textit{integration}. A paper's real status is not defined by editorial acceptance but by the degree to which it becomes embedded in knowledge flows. Let $\mathcal{R}(A)$ denote the reuse graph of $A$, consisting of downstream papers, derivative models, applied datasets, and teaching materials that incorporate or extend $A$. The rate of integration $\lambda_A(t)$ is then the derivative of the reuse curve:
\[
\lambda_A(t) = \frac{d|\mathcal{R}(A)|}{dt}.
\]
This is a more meaningful epistemic signal than any impact factor, representing the real uptake of the research into active domains of inquiry.

Modern scholarship increasingly depends on reuse and reinterpretation over time. For example, in machine learning research, new models are integrated into pipelines, frameworks, and software architectures—often within weeks of preprint dissemination. A static, terminal publication model fails to capture this dynamism. Studies show that preprints in high-velocity fields receive more citations than their published counterparts within the same year \citep{serghiou2018preprint}, indicating that integration, not formal acceptance, drives value.

Moreover, integration permits reversibility and refinement. A claim in $A$ may be corrected, superseded, or retracted. In a publish-and-freeze model, such corrections are awkwardly appended or buried. In a continuous model, revisions $\Delta A_i$ are registered as first-class objects with their own commentary and linkage structures. Let $A^{(i)}$ be the $i$-th revision of $A$, each version anchored to a hash $H(A^{(i)})$ and timestamp $t_i$. The active version of $A$ at time $t$ is then:
\[
A_{\text{live}}(t) = \arg\max_i \{ t_i \mid t_i \leq t \text{ and } A^{(i)} \in \mathcal{V}_A \},
\]
where $\mathcal{V}_A$ is the validated version set. This permits AI agents and reviewers to comment on, replicate, and trace specific versions rather than relying on decontextualised citations.

In this paradigm, reviewers do not merely decide on inclusion—they provide \textit{differential evaluations} that contribute to the visibility and utility of a work. Each review, critique, or response becomes part of an active epistemic topology. Over time, a work's importance is determined by its trajectory through this network. Some works will plateau, others will bifurcate, and some will be subsumed. But all are visible, traceable, and revisable. The transition is from decision to trajectory.

Such a model has precedents in computer science and mathematics. Software development has long relied on version-controlled repositories with public commits, issue tracking, and integration graphs. The analogous scholarly system is one where a paper is a node in a distributed graph of discourse, forkable, commentable, and composable. AI systems, such as those using scholarly knowledge graphs (e.g., Microsoft Academic Graph, Semantic Scholar Open Research Corpus), are already beginning to measure trajectory over static citation \citep{wang2019open}, further underscoring the shift from evaluation to integration.

In sum, to begin knowledge at publication is to restore its dynamism. Acceptance is no longer an endpoint; it is the instantiation of accountability. The scholarly object is born—not certified—when it is made public. Its merit is not editorial, but evolutionary.






\section{Cross-Disciplinary Application}

The rigidity of current scholarly infrastructure impedes the propagation and synthesis of knowledge across disciplines. Each academic field operates under an implicit schema $(\mathcal{L}_d, \mathcal{M}_d)$, where $\mathcal{L}_d$ denotes the domain-specific lexicon and $\mathcal{M}_d$ the accepted methodological canon. These domains, indexed over $d \in \{1, \dots, D\}$, often share conceptual or analytical structures yet fail to exchange insights due to incompatible validation mechanisms, editorial boundaries, and epistemic insularity.

A peer review system restructured as a public, persistent commentary ledger enables the reification of scholarly artefacts not as domain-isolated objects, but as semantically linkable, reusable modules. Let $A$ be a publication anchored to a distributed identity-validated review graph $\mathcal{G}_A$. Through structured metadata—ontological tagging, method classification, semantic embeddings—$A$ becomes searchable, interpretable, and commentable across all fields in which its methodology, data structures, or logical forms are relevant.

Mathematically, we define a cross-disciplinary linkage function $\chi: \mathcal{K}_i \times \mathcal{K}_j \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ that maps between epistemic objects in different domains $\mathcal{K}_i$ and $\mathcal{K}_j$ based on structural similarity, reuse patterns, or methodological analogues. AI agents, trained on structured review corpora and multi-field embeddings, can compute $\chi$ dynamically and with increasing precision over time. This allows a critique in field $i$ to propagate as a hypothesis in field $j$, creating transdisciplinary commentary trails.

For example, a statistical regularisation method developed in econometrics (e.g., Lasso regression) may receive methodological critique $\phi_i$ in $\mathcal{G}_A$ by a statistician. That same critique, when expressed in structured form and hashed to an open commentary ledger, becomes retrievable and integrable in biomedical contexts where Lasso is applied for feature selection in gene expression studies \citep{tibshirani1996regression, yuan2006model}. The review is no longer bounded by the field of publication but becomes a portable epistemic object, verifiable across contexts.

Furthermore, disciplines with limited experimental replicability—such as philosophy, law, or literary theory—gain epistemic traction not through repeated trials, but through interpretative reuse and critical discourse. The commentary ledger enables these forms of engagement to be quantified. Let $\mathcal{I}_d(A)$ be the integration function for domain $d$, defined as the volume and diversity of structured, identity-linked reuses of $A$ in arguments, syllabi, derivative essays, or legal citations. A philosophical argument that triggers $\sum_d \mathcal{I}_d(A) \gg 1$ becomes validated through intellectual uptake, even absent experimental falsifiability.

Importantly, this model introduces a new incentive architecture. Under the existing paradigm, interdisciplinary work is penalised for failing to meet canonical expectations in any single domain \citep{rafols2012diversity}. In the open review model, a work that triggers valid, structured commentaries across diverse domains accrues epistemic capital irrespective of initial reception. A physicist’s critique of econometric assumptions, if accurate and useful, becomes weighted over time via its reuse and impact, not its disciplinary origin.

The same applies in reverse: a legal scholar’s commentary on probabilistic reasoning in forensic science becomes a node in the scientific epistemic graph. The identity-linkage layer ensures that $\phi$, once published, retains attribution and can be reweighted as the context shifts. What matters is not field but contribution. Not authorship by gatekeeping but authorship by effect.

This model is compatible with the direction of contemporary AI-driven research classification. Transformer-based language models trained on mixed-discipline corpora can detect semantic analogues at scale \citep{cachan2023crossfield}. These models, when linked to a commentary ledger, provide domain translation surfaces: mappings from terms, patterns, or methodological primitives in one field to structurally equivalent or epistemically adjacent terms in another.

In total, the structured commentary model replaces disciplinary barriers with identity-linked, AI-readable, permanently attached interpretations. Knowledge becomes cumulative across vectors of reuse. The paper ceases to be a disciplinary artefact and becomes an epistemic vector in a shared knowledge manifold. What is reviewed in one place can be recomposed in another. The result is a system built not on mutual exclusion, but on recursive integration.


\subsection{Natural and Computational Sciences: Replicability and Data Disclosure}

In the natural and computational sciences, the core epistemic test of a claim is replicability. A proposition $\psi$ validated in experiment or computation must yield the same result under independently reproduced conditions. Let $\mathcal{E}(\psi, D)$ denote an experiment $\mathcal{E}$ testing proposition $\psi$ over dataset $D$. The replicability of $\psi$ is a function $R(\psi) = \mathbb{P}[\mathcal{E}_i(\psi, D') = \mathcal{E}_0(\psi, D)]$ across independent implementations $\mathcal{E}_i$ using variant datasets $D'$. A publication claiming $\psi$ must therefore include both the original protocol and all data elements sufficient to instantiate $\mathcal{E}_0$.

Traditional publication models fail this standard. Journals often permit, but do not require, publication of code, raw data, or even the parameter choices underlying computational models \citep{ioannidis2005most}. Peer reviewers lack both time and tooling to execute replication. Worse, claims are accepted on narrative plausibility rather than mechanistic traceability. This has led to persistent replication crises across psychology, biology, and computational social science \citep{begley2012drug, open2015estimating}. A review without access to $D$ and $\mathcal{E}$ is non-epistemic—it affirms style, not truth.

A restructured peer review model mandates structured disclosure. Every artefact $A$ representing an empirical claim is associated with a triple $(D, \mathcal{E}, \phi)$: data, executable protocol, and review commentary. These components are hashed and referenced immutably:
\[
H_A = H(D \| \mathcal{E} \| \phi)
\]
with each component stored via content-addressable systems (e.g., IPFS) and the hash $H_A$ anchored to a public blockchain (e.g., BSV) to prevent post hoc alteration. The epistemic claim becomes reconstructible—reviewers and readers can validate not only whether $\psi$ was claimed, but whether it was computed or measured correctly.

AI systems enhance this model. Trained over structured disclosures, they parse $\mathcal{E}$ into execution graphs, validate data-type integrity in $D$, check coherence between hypothesis structure and analysis, and detect undocumented analytic flexibility (so-called $p$-hacking) \citep{huntington2019machine}. Automated differential replication, wherein $\mathcal{E}$ is re-run over synthetic or variant datasets $D'$, becomes feasible at scale. This allows trust weightings on $\psi$ to be computed dynamically as:
\[
T(\psi, t) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \delta(\mathcal{E}_i(D'_i), \mathcal{E}_0(D))
\]
where $\delta$ measures result congruence and $n$ is the number of independent executions.

For example, in computational biology, the reliability of gene expression classifiers depends not only on initial accuracy but on generalisability across cohorts. A study claiming 95\% classification accuracy on cohort $D$ must be evaluated through a continuous integration model, where new datasets $D'$ are automatically tested using the published $\mathcal{E}$, and results $\psi'$ compared. Each test generates a structured review entry, signed by the executing agent, and appended to the commentary ledger. The scientific object becomes \textit{self-replicating}: each use is a verification.

Furthermore, structured disclosure transforms negative results from editorial liabilities into high-value epistemic signals. If $\psi$ fails under ten distinct $D'_i$, this is evidence of boundary conditions or false discovery. Current systems bury such evidence. The commentary ledger makes these failures permanent, citeable, and machine-analysable. Epistemic falsification becomes additive, not subtractive.

Crucially, structured data disclosure supports non-anonymised accountability. Each commentary $\phi$ is cryptographically signed and indexed under an identity key $k_{\alpha}$ with a historical record of prior evaluations, replicability scores, and methodological validity. This allows trust in $\psi$ to incorporate not only the number of replications, but the epistemic weight of those performing them. An agent with a history of precise, correct replications contributes more to $T(\psi)$ than one with ambiguous or contradictory output.

This structure answers the replication crisis not through more journals or stricter p-values, but through a distributed ledger of executable critique. Claims are not final—they are continuously tested, verified, or corrected. The record of science becomes one of code and consequence, not formatting and favour.


\subsection{Humanities and Law: Interpretative Use and Impact Trace}

In the humanities and legal scholarship, knowledge does not evolve through replication or falsification, but through structured argumentation, interpretative reuse, and critical integration. The epistemic value of an artefact $A$—be it a philosophical argument, judicial analysis, or literary interpretation—lies in its ability to enter the discursive matrix of ongoing thought. Let $U(A)$ denote the use function of $A$, mapping to a set of identifiable interpretative acts $\{\theta_1, \theta_2, \ldots, \theta_n\}$, each performed by a qualified peer. The density and depth of this set serve as an empirical proxy for intellectual impact.

Traditional peer review systems inadequately model this process. The fate of $A$ depends upon its alignment with editorial and disciplinary expectations rather than the long-term pattern of its argumentative integration. Published interpretations or critiques of $A$—especially outside the journal of original appearance—remain weakly linked, often uncited, and epistemically invisible. There is no durable trace of how $A$ reshapes or is reshaped by the interpretative community.

In a structured public commentary system, each $\theta_i$—an act of interpretative use—is logged, signed, and indexed against $A$. Let each $\theta_i$ be represented as a tuple $(k_{\alpha_i}, \tau_i, \phi_i, \mu_i)$ where $k_{\alpha_i}$ is the identity of the interpreter, $\tau_i$ the timestamp, $\phi_i$ the interpretative claim, and $\mu_i$ a modality descriptor (e.g., application, critique, synthesis, contradiction). These are appended to an immutable ledger, producing a commentary trace $\mathcal{C}_A = \{\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_n\}$ analogous to a citation graph but enriched with structured semantics.

This framework aligns with jurisprudential analysis, where the force of legal reasoning is cumulative. A judicial precedent $P$ is not confirmed by replication, but by interpretative elaboration, adoption in related cases, and challenge in appellate proceedings. Each of these interactions can be formalised as commentary events $\theta_i$ with structured linkages to $P$, producing an impact trace $\mathcal{I}_P(t)$ over time. Courts, legal scholars, and policy analysts contribute to $\mathcal{I}_P$ through identity-signed legal commentary, permitting empirical computation of interpretative salience.

Let the influence of $A$ at time $t$ be defined as:
\[
I(A, t) = \sum_{i=1}^{n_t} w_i \cdot s(\phi_i),
\]
where $s(\phi_i)$ quantifies semantic divergence and argumentative novelty, and $w_i$ weights reviewer trust and reuse visibility. Unlike traditional citation counts, $I(A, t)$ captures interpretative depth. A minor citation in a footnote does not equal a substantive critique or a reframing of method. Structured commentary makes such distinctions quantifiable.

Moreover, the use of identity-linked records mitigates the endemic anonymity that pervades conference reviews and gatekeeping in humanities publishing. Each contributor to $\mathcal{C}_A$ does so with persistent scholarly identity, allowing trust metrics to form based on historical coherence, reception, and methodological rigor. This is compatible with recent calls for open peer commentary in the humanities and social theory, where discursive accountability is foundational \citep{fitzpatrick2019generous}.

AI systems can trace $\mathcal{C}_A$ to detect interpretative lineage: how one argument $\phi_j$ evolves from or responds to $\phi_i$, enabling automated mapping of schools of thought or conceptual divergence. This formalises what hermeneutics calls the \textit{horizon of understanding}—a dynamic network of evolving positions, now rendered visible and auditable \citep{gadamer2004truth}.

Finally, such a model enables interpretative reuse outside of academia to be tracked. In law, public policy, journalism, and public discourse, arguments derived from $A$ can be structurally linked, provided they maintain verifiable anchors (e.g., quotations, metadata, or citation fingerprints). These become off-domain nodes in $\mathcal{C}_A$, extending epistemic valuation beyond the academy.

In sum, the value of a humanities or legal text is the density and diversity of its interpretative use. Replication is irrelevant; impact is relational. By encoding every interpretative act as a structured, identity-signed commentary, we transform reception from anecdote to traceable epistemology. Scholarship becomes not merely publication, but participation.


\subsection{Social Sciences: Methodological Transfer and Use-Signal Generation}

The epistemology of the social sciences occupies a liminal zone between experimental reproducibility and interpretative synthesis. While empirical validation remains critical, many social science contributions are valued for their methodological innovation and downstream applicability across fields. Let $A$ be a methodological artefact—such as a coding scheme, network model, survey instrument, or analytic protocol—originating in one subdomain. The epistemic value of $A$ lies not merely in its internal results but in its transposability: its ability to generate valid knowledge when redeployed in structurally distinct contexts.

We define the \textit{methodological transfer function} $T_A(t)$ as the cumulative set of structured reuses of $A$ across domains, such that each transfer instance $\tau_i$ satisfies:
\[
\tau_i = (k_{\alpha_i}, D_i, \mathcal{E}_i, \psi_i),
\]
where $k_{\alpha_i}$ is the identity of the researcher, $D_i$ the new data context, $\mathcal{E}_i$ the application protocol, and $\psi_i$ the resulting claim. Each $\tau_i$ is cryptographically signed, hashed, and stored on a distributed ledger, forming a traceable history of application. The growth of $T_A(t)$ reflects not just popularity, but epistemic transportability and validity across sociocultural systems.

This model addresses a core limitation of current publication norms. Methodological artefacts are often under-recognised when used outside their disciplinary origin. For instance, Granovetter’s strength-of-weak-ties theory \citep{granovetter1973strength} migrated from sociology into economics, epidemiology, and computer science, yet citation analysis fails to differentiate derivative reuse from shallow referencing. A commentary-led system resolves this by capturing structured application logs, each with defined semantic relationships to $A$: adaptation, extension, replication, or critique.

To formalise reuse, we define a \textit{use-signal vector} $\vec{U}_A = (u_1, u_2, \ldots, u_n)$ where each $u_i$ corresponds to a structured commentary event with weight $w_i$, computed as a function of argument complexity, semantic novelty, domain distance, and validation integrity. Let $S_A(t)$ be the use signal score:
\[
S_A(t) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i,
\]
which becomes the core scalar indicator of $A$'s epistemic traction. Unlike citation counts, $S_A(t)$ distinguishes between superficial mention and deep methodological integration.

AI systems play a key role in extracting and evaluating $u_i$ instances from full-text corpora, preprint repositories, or structured commentary databases. Using domain-adaptive transformers trained on social science literature, AI agents can detect method reuse, identify semantic transformations, and distinguish between conceptual inheritance and literal replication \citep{beltagy2019scibert}. These capabilities enable dynamic updating of $T_A(t)$ and automatic weighting of $u_i$ events within $\vec{U}_A$.

Moreover, the social sciences frequently confront the problem of context dependency—methods that succeed in one population may fail in another. The commentary ledger model permits visibility into these divergences. Failed transfers, reinterpretations, or adjustments to $A$ are recorded explicitly, allowing meta-analysis of failure conditions and the emergence of methodological boundary maps. As \citet{cartwright2007nature} argues, causality in the social sciences is local and material-dependent; generalisability is a hypothesis, not a default. Open commentary transforms this from an obstacle to a feature: each failed transfer contributes to the epistemic topology of $A$.

Crucially, identity-linked commentary structures disincentivise silent borrowing. Currently, methods are frequently reimplemented or marginally modified without proper attribution. In a system where each reuse is verifiably signed and rewarded via epistemic capital, attribution becomes economically rational and ethically enforced. Scholars who develop methodologically robust artefacts receive credit not only at publication, but each time their work informs new results.

Finally, the commentary model captures interdisciplinary transits. A political scientist’s survey instrument may find use in computational linguistics for bias detection; a geographer’s spatial clustering metric may become foundational in network epidemiology. Traditional publication models render these transitions opaque. With structured commentary, each $\tau_i$ becomes a node in a reusability graph, visualisable, queryable, and analysable by AI. This reframes methodological value as a function of integration, not isolation.

In sum, social science methods achieve epistemic status through diffusion, contextual success, and interpretative extension. The commentary ledger makes each step in that process explicit, traceable, and accountable. What matters is not where a method originates, but how far—and how faithfully—it travels.






\section{Technological Infrastructure}

The transformation of peer review into an open, identity-anchored, and commentary-driven system necessitates a technological substrate capable of preserving integrity, ensuring transparency, and enabling large-scale automation. This infrastructure must satisfy five non-negotiable conditions:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
  \item \textbf{Immutability}: Once published, commentary and associated review metadata cannot be altered or deleted.
  \item \textbf{Identity linkage}: Every assertion must be cryptographically bound to a persistent scholarly identity.
  \item \textbf{Append-only history}: Intellectual updates, corrections, and refutations are not overwritten but layered as versioned amendments.
  \item \textbf{Semantic structure}: All contributions must be machine-readable for effective AI-assisted synthesis and validation.
  \item \textbf{Protocol stability}: The platform must be governed by a fixed, auditable rule set over time to guarantee verification of historical records.
\end{enumerate}

These requirements are not met by current academic infrastructure, which relies on mutable web servers, non-verifiable peer identities, and opaque editorial pipelines. The appropriate solution involves a modular integration of blockchain technology, distributed file systems, cryptographic signature schemes, and structured semantic formats.

Let the review system be modelled as a distributed ledger $\mathcal{L}$ with the following structure: each review event $\phi_i$ is hashed $h_i = H(\phi_i)$, signed with the key of the agent $k_{\alpha_i}$, and timestamped $\tau_i$. The tuple $(h_i, \mathrm{sig}_{k_{\alpha_i}}(\phi_i), \tau_i)$ is embedded in a blockchain transaction $T_i$ which references the original artefact $A$ via content hash $H(A)$. The network of such transactions forms a commentary graph $G = (V, E)$, where $V$ includes both artefacts and agents, and $E$ encodes the semantic and temporal structure of intellectual evaluation.

This technological model is layered. On-chain components ensure verifiability and permanence. Off-chain storage (e.g., IPFS, BitFS) hosts full-text commentary and supplementary materials. AI systems operate across this infrastructure to compute trust scores, identify interpretative divergences, trace method reuse, and detect structural conflicts. Crucially, the protocol must be fixed and auditable, with a low-fee, high-throughput architecture capable of anchoring the global research corpus without economic bottlenecks.

This infrastructure repositions scholarly publishing from a closed dissemination model to an open verification network. It enables not only commentary preservation, but intellectual trajectory mapping, reviewer accountability, and downstream integration analysis. In essence, it turns every scientific claim into a node in a public, provable, and continually evolving knowledge ledger.


\subsection{Blockchain for Immutable Commentary and Identity Linking}

At the foundation of a verifiable and accountable scholarly review system lies the requirement for an append-only, tamper-proof ledger that binds each intellectual assertion to a persistent identity. Blockchain—defined formally as a distributed, cryptographically linked sequence of transaction sets governed by a fixed protocol—satisfies this requirement with mathematical precision. Let $\mathcal{B}$ be such a blockchain, indexed by block height $h \in \mathbb{N}$, where each block $b_h$ contains a finite, ordered set of transactions $\{T_1, \ldots, T_n\}$. Each $T_i$ represents a signed, timestamped assertion anchored to a content hash of a review, critique, or commentary.

For any scholarly artefact $A$, its associated commentary $\phi_i$ is structured and signed as:
\[
\phi_i = \left(H(A), \text{content}, \mu_i, \tau_i, \text{sig}_{k_{\alpha_i}} \right),
\]
where $H(A)$ is the cryptographic digest of the original artefact, $\mu_i$ is a modality field (e.g., critique, replication, extension), $\tau_i$ the timestamp, and $k_{\alpha_i}$ the persistent public key of the reviewer $\alpha_i$. This payload is hashed as $h_i = H(\phi_i)$ and submitted to $\mathcal{B}$ via a transaction $T_i$. Once $T_i$ is mined into $b_h$, the assertion is immutable—any future repudiation, correction, or contradiction must be appended as a new transaction $T_j$ referencing $T_i$ via Merkle ancestry or explicit linkage.

Unlike centralised review platforms where moderation can delete or alter reviewer history, blockchain guarantees permanence. This immutability enables the epistemic auditability of review. Every correction, error, prediction, or methodological objection is preserved in an indelible public record, facilitating retrospective analysis and longitudinal trust modelling.

Identity linkage is enforced through public-key cryptography. Each reviewer or contributor $\alpha$ maintains a persistent keypair $(k_{\alpha}^{\text{pub}}, k_{\alpha}^{\text{priv}})$, with $\phi_i$ signed as:
\[
\text{sig}_{k_{\alpha}^{\text{priv}}}(\phi_i) = \sigma_i,
\]
which can be verified by any node in the network using $k_{\alpha}^{\text{pub}}$. Identity correlation is enhanced through external verification: academic credentials, institutional affiliations, and ORCID identifiers can be cryptographically bound to $k_{\alpha}$ through verified attestations. This prevents sockpuppet reviewing, eliminates anonymous sabotage, and allows reputation scores to be computed over time as functions of review quality, replicability outcomes, and scholarly integration.

For scalability and protocol stability, only blockchains with unbounded data throughput, deterministic validation rules, and low transaction fees are viable. Among existing implementations, the original Bitcoin protocol (as preserved in Bitcoin SV) satisfies these constraints: block sizes are limited only by miner configuration, the protocol is fixed (per Bitcoin white paper and subsequent clarification by Nakamoto), and transaction fees remain negligible. This permits global-scale anchoring of research artefacts and commentary, without economic filtering or rule instability.

The value of blockchain here is not speculative—it is epistemic. It transforms peer review from a private act of discretionary judgement into a public act of scholarly accountability. Assertions are no longer whispered behind editorial curtains but signed, witnessed, and preserved. Each reviewer becomes a visible part of the intellectual record, and each assertion an object of study. Scholarship shifts from reputation by gatekeeping to reputation by verifiable contribution.


\subsection{Append-Only Ledgers for Citation Traces}

In scholarly communication, citations function as the connective tissue of epistemic lineage. A citation is not merely a reference; it is an assertion of dependency, inheritance, extension, critique, or reuse. Current systems treat citations as static text—opaque, untyped, and non-verifiable. The epistemic structure of knowledge remains unmodelled: there is no canonical mechanism to trace, validate, or interrogate the specific function a citation performs within an argument. To reconstruct scholarly communication as a verifiable structure, citations must be formalised as cryptographically anchored, semantically typed events in an append-only ledger.

Let $A$ be a citing work and $B$ a cited work. A citation event $\gamma_{A \rightarrow B}$ is defined as:
\[
\gamma_{A \rightarrow B} = \left( H(A), H(B), \mu, \tau, \text{sig}_{k_{\alpha}} \right),
\]
where $H(\cdot)$ denotes the cryptographic content hash, $\mu$ is the citation modality (e.g., foundational, critique, replication, methodological reuse), $\tau$ the timestamp, and $k_{\alpha}$ the signing key of the author $\alpha$ asserting the citation. This tuple is hashed and appended to an append-only ledger $\mathcal{L}_\gamma$, either directly on-chain (e.g., in BSV via OP\_RETURN) or off-chain with on-chain anchoring of Merkle roots.

This structure provides three core epistemic guarantees:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
  \item \textbf{Verifiability}: Any claim of dependency from $A$ to $B$ is publicly auditable and cryptographically bound to the signer.
  \item \textbf{Modality visibility}: Each citation is not a neutral count but a typed relationship, enabling automated mapping of intellectual influence.
  \item \textbf{Temporal integrity}: The order of citation events is provable and irreversible, supporting longitudinal analysis.
\end{enumerate}

AI agents trained on structured scientific corpora can parse full texts to extract $\gamma_{A \rightarrow B}$ tuples, infer latent modalities using citation intent classifiers \citep{jurgens2018citation}, and cross-validate with ledger entries. This enables the construction of a semantically enriched citation graph $G = (V, E)$ where nodes $V$ are artefacts and edges $E$ are typed, timestamped citation events. This graph, when anchored in an append-only ledger, resists both retroactive distortion and selective omission—common pitfalls in current citation databases.

Further, append-only citation ledgers allow for epistemic anomaly detection. For instance, a citation spike following publication may be parsed by AI to distinguish between supportive reuse, coordinated rebuttal, or social signal cascades. Works that accrue high counts of $\gamma$ entries with contradiction modalities, for example, may be flagged for closer analysis or correction. Knowledge evaluation becomes dynamic, not merely cumulative.

In legal scholarship and precedent analysis, such a system transforms citation into jurisprudential structure. Let $P$ be a precedent case and $C_i$ a citing opinion. The ledger $\mathcal{L}_\gamma$ captures not just that $C_i$ cited $P$, but that it did so in the modality of extension, limitation, or overruling. These entries allow AI to trace legal doctrine evolution as a semantic DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph), with full audit trails and historical reversibility.

Moreover, scholarly contributions can be evaluated not only on citation volume but on influence topology. Let $I(B)$ be the influence score of artefact $B$:
\[
I(B) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} w(\gamma_{A_i \rightarrow B}),
\]
where $w(\gamma)$ is a weight function over modality, author credibility, and downstream reuse. Because each $\gamma$ is signed and typed, this function can be computed recursively and adaptively—allowing more granular, fair, and epistemically valid evaluations than traditional impact factors or $h$-indices.

Finally, append-only citation ledgers ensure attribution integrity. Misattribution, omitted credit, or ambiguous references are refutable once all assertions are anchored with cryptographic linkage. The ledger provides a canonical record of who cited whom, for what reason, and when. This supports accountability, combats scholarly parasitism, and provides the necessary infrastructure for citation-based compensation or incentive models.

In sum, an append-only citation ledger transforms citation from a bibliographic afterthought into a structured epistemic transaction. It encodes the mechanics of knowledge inheritance and critique with cryptographic rigour, making scholarship computable, traceable, and immune to revisionist distortion.


\subsection{Versioning, Addenda, and Formal Corrections}

In any system of knowledge production, revision is not an exception—it is an epistemic imperative. Scientific claims, interpretative arguments, and methodological frameworks evolve through error identification, refinement, and contextual reanalysis. Yet current scholarly publishing systems treat corrections and updates as marginal events, hidden in corrigenda, errata, or informal post-publication commentary. This results in epistemic fragmentation, where readers lack a canonical trail linking a published work to its subsequent evolution. To rectify this, versioning, addenda, and corrections must be formalised as first-class, identity-linked, and immutable components within the scholarly record.

Let $A^{(0)}$ be the initial publication of a scholarly artefact. We define its epistemic lifecycle as a sequence of signed versions $\{A^{(0)}, A^{(1)}, A^{(2)}, \ldots, A^{(n)}\}$, where each $A^{(i)}$ is a discrete revision, correction, or extension. Each version is hashed $H(A^{(i)})$, timestamped $\tau_i$, and submitted to a version ledger $\mathcal{V}_A$, which satisfies the append-only property:
\[
\mathcal{V}_A = \left\{ \left(H(A^{(i)}), \tau_i, \text{sig}_{k_{\alpha}}, \mu_i \right) \right\}_{i=0}^n,
\]
where $\mu_i$ specifies the modification type: corrigendum, retraction, addendum, reanalysis, or extension. The result is a chronologically ordered, cryptographically anchored chain of custody for every knowledge object.

Unlike traditional systems where metadata about revisions is opaque and editorially controlled, a public, identity-linked ledger renders all epistemic changes visible and audit-worthy. Each modification must be signed by an authorised agent $k_{\alpha}$ (e.g., the original author, journal editor, or qualified third-party replicator), and indexed in the ledger with formal semantic structure. This transforms version control from ad hoc PDF replacement to a verifiable intellectual history.

From a computational perspective, this structure enables AI systems to reason over the differential content of each $A^{(i)}$. Let $\Delta_i = A^{(i)} - A^{(i-1)}$ be the semantic delta between consecutive versions. Natural language models and symbolic diff tools can classify $\Delta_i$ as minor (e.g., typographical fix), major (e.g., method correction), or critical (e.g., falsification of results). These classifications can be weighted into trust metrics $T(A, t)$ over time:
\[
T(A, t) = f\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n} \delta_i \cdot w_i \right),
\]
where $\delta_i$ encodes severity and $w_i$ is the epistemic weight of the correcting agent. A paper that issues a formal retraction followed by silence will have its $T$ decay; one that issues successive clarifications and improvements may see $T$ increase.

Critically, formal corrections must be linked back to all downstream artefacts that cite or reuse $A^{(i)}$. Let $C(A^{(i)}) = \{B_1, \ldots, B_m\}$ be the set of citing works. Upon entry of a correction $\Delta_j$, AI agents traverse the citation graph to update affected nodes and notify authors or readers of inherited epistemic changes. This backward propagation ensures that derivative work is not built on silently corrupted foundations.

This process is not theoretical—it has been partially implemented in software development environments (e.g., Git, distributed version control systems), but absent from academic publishing. Systems such as CrossMark and Retraction Watch provide surface-level updates, but lack cryptographic guarantees, machine readability, and integration into downstream dependency chains. A blockchain-anchored $\mathcal{V}_A$ with structured diff metadata, semantic tagging, and AI integration remedies this omission.

Finally, the system enables reversible epistemology. If a claim once retracted is later validated, the ledger allows full visibility into its reversal, with cryptographic proofs of authorship, justification, and date of rehabilitation. This supports the dynamic, adversarial character of science: not infallibility, but revision under scrutiny.

In conclusion, versioning, addenda, and formal corrections are not metadata—they are the mechanism by which scholarship earns trust. By encoding each change as a traceable, immutable, identity-bound event, we transform scientific self-correction from embarrassment into virtue, and from anecdote into algorithm.





\section{AI as Review Agent and Synthesis Tool}

The integration of artificial intelligence into scholarly infrastructure marks a categorical shift in the epistemic architecture of science. Where human reviewers operate with bounded rationality, institutional constraints, and limited attention bandwidth, AI systems offer scalable, continuous, and structurally unbiased forms of knowledge engagement. These agents do not replace human judgement—they augment it, operationalising critique, synthesis, and validation across orders of magnitude.

Let $\mathcal{A}$ denote an AI agent embedded in the commentary ecosystem. Its core functions include parsing scholarly artefacts, identifying semantic dependencies, classifying citation intent, verifying methodological reproducibility, and mapping cross-disciplinary analogues. Formally, for any artefact $A$, $\mathcal{A}$ computes a knowledge vector $\vec{K}_A$, derived from structured content analysis, citation topology, commentary trace mining, and external corpus embeddings. This vector is used to compute: (i) latent topic models, (ii) epistemic novelty scores, and (iii) semantic similarity mappings across fields.

Crucially, the AI system interfaces directly with the identity-linked, append-only commentary ledger. This permits provable attribution, version-aware content analysis, and live integration of new structured reviews. It also ensures that all outputs of $\mathcal{A}$—whether summaries, conflict detections, or epistemic valuations—are traceable, reproducible, and contestable.

The role of AI is not merely to accelerate peer review but to transform its ontology. Instead of isolated verdicts, review becomes a distributed, recursive, multi-modal process—anchored in signed assertions, revised through probabilistic reasoning, and navigated via computational topology. The future of scholarly validation lies not in editorial consensus, but in the provable trajectory of thought, as rendered legible by machine reasoning.

\subsection{Summarisation, Citation Mapping, and Cross-Disciplinary Analysis}

Artificial intelligence systems, properly embedded within a cryptographically anchored scholarly infrastructure, offer the capacity to abstract, synthesise, and recompose knowledge beyond the cognitive horizon of individual researchers or disciplinary enclaves. This subsection defines the functional role of AI in the compression of argument, extraction of semantic dependency, and generation of cross-domain relational indices in a way that is mathematically bounded, epistemically rigorous, and identity-linked.

Let an AI review agent $\mathcal{A}$ operate over a corpus $\mathcal{C} = \{A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n\}$ of scholarly artefacts, each $A_i$ associated with metadata $\mathcal{M}_i$, authorship identity key $k_{\alpha_i}$, and a timestamp $\tau_i$. For each artefact $A_i$, the agent computes a structured summary $S_i = \mathcal{A}_s(A_i)$, where $\mathcal{A}_s$ is a deterministic or probabilistically bounded summarisation operator that preserves core argumentation, methodological structure, and result typology. The fidelity of $S_i$ is verified via cryptographic anchoring: the digest $H(S_i)$ is signed by the agent and appended to the commentary ledger, allowing future recalibration and audit.

Citation mapping is constructed via a directed weighted graph $G = (V, E)$ where each node $v_i \in V$ corresponds to an artefact $A_i$ and each edge $e_{ij} \in E$ encodes a citation from $A_i$ to $A_j$. Let $w_{ij}$ denote a function mapping the edge to a vector space embedding that reflects intent ($\iota$), polarity ($\pi$), and contextual integration depth ($\delta$):
\[
w_{ij} = f(\iota_{ij}, \pi_{ij}, \delta_{ij}).
\]
Each vector component is derived from a contextual transformer-based parse of the citing text, embedded within a reproducible attention-weighted encoder architecture. This enables downstream tasks such as citation distortion detection, discovery of structural redundancy, and identification of high-entropy citation clusters—zones of potential theoretical or empirical instability.

Cross-disciplinary analysis is structured around latent space projections. Define for each artefact $A_i$ a feature embedding $\vec{\phi_i} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ derived from content semantics, methodological signature, and citation context. A field projection operator $\mathcal{P}_F$ projects each $\vec{\phi_i}$ onto the basis of a canonical disciplinary space $F$, allowing comparison between otherwise non-interoperable domains. The cosine similarity $\cos(\vec{\phi_i}, \mathcal{P}_F(\vec{\phi_j}))$ provides a scalar measure of latent conceptual overlap across disciplines, enabling AI systems to detect epistemic convergence where textual or institutional siloes have historically obscured synthesis.

Importantly, every operation performed by $\mathcal{A}$ is logged with cryptographic identity. No summary, embedding, citation relation, or field projection is accepted into the system without verifiable attribution. Each AI agent signs its operations, stores the hash alongside model parameters, training data hashes, and execution logs, thus ensuring that synthesis itself is subject to review, audit, and correction. This prevents hallucination, protects against malicious inference bias, and ensures that summarisation becomes an epistemic act of record—not inference without provenance.

In total, AI summarisation and mapping within a commentary-led infrastructure does not simply scale human review; it reconfigures it. Argument is made legible. Impact is made traceable. Disciplinary divides are made permeable. This is not artificial intelligence in service of convenience—it is structured cognition at scale, grounded in formal identity, immutable auditability, and epistemic accountability.

\subsection{Flagging Contradictions, Overlaps, and Novelty}

In a commentary-led, identity-linked scholarly infrastructure, contradiction, redundancy, and novelty are not editorial judgments but measurable epistemic configurations. Artificial intelligence, when appropriately embedded, performs automated theorem conflict detection, model comparison, and semantic novelty estimation across a global citation-anchored corpus. The role of AI is to instantiate formal detection operators that render hidden relations and epistemic misalignments legible—thereby reinforcing structural coherence and amplifying true innovation.

Let $\mathcal{C} = \{A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n\}$ denote a corpus of peer-reviewed artefacts, each with an associated vector representation $\vec{\phi}_i$ in semantic space $\mathbb{R}^d$, a formal model component $\mathcal{F}_i$ (where applicable), and timestamped commentary thread $\Gamma_i$. Define an AI contradiction operator $\mathcal{K}$ such that:
\[
\mathcal{K}(A_i, A_j) = 
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if } \exists\, (\psi \in \mathcal{F}_i, \neg\psi \in \mathcal{F}_j) \\
0, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
This evaluates direct logical contradiction through machine-interpretable claim formalisation. In practical terms, $\mathcal{K}$ may employ first-order logic, statistical model contradiction (e.g. opposing regression slopes on shared predictors), or hypothesis test incongruities over overlapping datasets. When $\mathcal{K} = 1$, a flag is attached to both $A_i$ and $A_j$, linked through a contradiction token stored in the ledger, signed by the detecting agent, and open to community review and contextual override.

Overlap detection uses proximity metrics in semantic and methodological spaces. Let $D_S(A_i, A_j) = \|\vec{\phi}_i - \vec{\phi}_j\|$ denote semantic distance and $D_M(A_i, A_j)$ the divergence between methods $\mathcal{M}_i$ and $\mathcal{M}_j$ as operationalised by procedure encoding (e.g. protocol graphs or statistical blueprint embeddings). A flag for redundancy is triggered when:
\[
D_S(A_i, A_j) < \epsilon_s \quad \text{and} \quad D_M(A_i, A_j) < \epsilon_m,
\]
where $\epsilon_s, \epsilon_m$ are domain-tuned thresholds calibrated over citation centrality and temporal lag. Importantly, such overlap is not punitive—it signals an opportunity for synthesis, reduction, or metanalysis. Repetition is not failure; unacknowledged redundancy is.

Novelty, by contrast, is determined not by absence of citations or lexical rarity but by deviation in the conditional knowledge graph. Define the posterior knowledge graph $G_t$ as the structure of validated relations up to time $t$. Let $A_k$ be a new contribution. The novelty score $\nu(A_k)$ is a composite function:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\nu(A_k) =\ & \lambda_1 \cdot \texttt{graph-entropy}(\vec{\phi}_k, G_t)\ + \\
            & \lambda_2 \cdot \texttt{claim-distance}(\mathcal{F}_k, \{\mathcal{F}_j\}_{j \neq k})\ + \\
            & \lambda_3 \cdot \texttt{commentary-latency}(A_k)
\end{aligned}
\]

where the first term measures surprise against existing knowledge structure, the second quantifies claim deviation, and the third penalises publication delay in rapidly moving epistemic fronts. All weights $\lambda_i$ are domain-tuned. Outputs are appended to the artefact's ledger hash and reflected in downstream discoverability ranking, reader alerts, and citation weighting.

Identity plays a central role in trust propagation. Every flagged contradiction or overlap is linked to both the detecting agent and the artefacts’ originators, preserving accountability. Authors may respond, rebut, or revise; such actions are similarly linked and immutably recorded. False positives are not silent failures—they are visible and correctable within the open ledger. AI does not decide—AI proposes. Community and formal traceability decide.

This system ensures that scholarly claims exist not as isolated textual artefacts but as modular epistemic assertions subject to live alignment with a continuously updating global structure. Novelty is proven, not postured. Contradictions are tracked, not erased. Redundancies are resolved, not buried. In this structure, knowledge becomes self-checking, self-documenting, and epistemically precise.

\subsection{Agent-Based Review and Multi-Metric Valuation}

The atomisation of peer review into distributed, computational agents enables an epistemic shift from gatekeeping to dynamic, identity-linked valuation. In a traditional review pipeline, valuation is binary and opaque—accept or reject—mediated by unaccountable, anonymous judgement. In contrast, agent-based review disaggregates evaluation into modular, continuous functions: methodological audit, claim validation, relevance scoring, interpretive trace analysis, and identity-weighted influence attribution. These operations are performed in parallel, logged immutably, and bound to both the agent's provenance and the contributor’s scholarly record.

Let $\mathcal{A}_r = \{\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \dots, \alpha_m\}$ be a population of autonomous review agents. Each agent $\alpha_j$ is a bounded evaluator with a domain-specific ontology $\Omega_j$, credential weight $\kappa_j$, and audit signature key $k_j$. For any artefact $A$, the evaluation process produces a multi-dimensional score vector:
\[
\vec{v}_A = \left[ v^{(1)}_A, v^{(2)}_A, \dots, v^{(n)}_A \right],
\]
where each $v^{(i)}_A$ represents a distinct evaluative dimension (e.g.\ reproducibility, logical coherence, empirical novelty, methodological rigour, downstream influence potential). Each dimension $i$ is evaluated via a function $f^{(i)}_j$ such that:
\[
v^{(i)}_A = \frac{1}{\sum_j \kappa_j} \sum_j \kappa_j \cdot f^{(i)}_j(A \mid \Omega_j).
\]

These valuations are anchored to the artefact’s commentary ledger entry, signed by the contributing agents and indexed for discoverability. Critically, agent identities are not anonymised. Each $\alpha_j$ has a persistent scholarly key linked to their own history of evaluations, reviews, and contradictions. This recursive traceability creates an ecosystem where reviewer quality becomes measurable: the predictive power of past valuations, the accuracy of contradiction flags, the uptake and replication of positively scored works—all inform an agent’s trust score, which in turn modulates $\kappa_j$.

Multi-metric valuation supports multiple downstream systems:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Weighted citation indices:} A citation from a positively scored artefact with high reviewer trust propagates more influence than one from a retracted or low-impact paper.
  \item \textbf{Ranking and discovery:} Artefacts are surfaced not solely by citation count, but by a composite of review scores, commentary consensus, novelty vectors, and engagement latency.
  \item \textbf{Correction incentive structures:} Authors and agents gain trust when they self-correct, disambiguate misinterpretations, or engage constructively in contradiction resolution—each action appended to the trace ledger.
\end{itemize}

Further, agents may be specialised or generalist. Some operate at the level of field-specific audit (e.g.\ statistical model integrity in biomedical research), others engage in meta-structural trace synthesis (e.g.\ flagging convergences in independent domains). Agent overlap is by design—plural valuations are expected, and divergence becomes an epistemic feature, not a flaw. Contradictory agent outputs invoke resolution protocols—ledger-stored dialogues, community challenges, and differential trust application.

Mathematically, this architecture instantiates a partially observable epistemic valuation process over a dynamic corpus, bound to cryptographic identity. The transition matrix of scholarly influence becomes not a black box of editorial will but a transparent function of distributed, accountable computation.

Agent-based review reframes peer evaluation as an ongoing, multi-scalar, reputation-linked process. Not publication, but valuation. Not binary verdicts, but scalar epistemics. Not anonymity, but identity—anchored, cumulative, and subject to review itself.



\section{Identity-Linked Revision and Epistemic Traceability}

In traditional academic systems, revision is reactive, obscured, and often reputationally damaging. Retractions are stigmatised, null results remain unpublished, and negative findings are institutionally filtered out of the epistemic record. This structure enforces a false teleology of progress, where only affirmations of hypothesis and continuation of disciplinary consensus are retained. The consequence is not only an epistemological skew, but also a distortion in the topology of scholarly knowledge. What is absent is often more informative than what remains. In contrast, a model built on identity-linked revision and epistemic traceability reconfigures correction, retraction, and null discovery as primary contributions—not flaws.

Let each scholarly artefact $A_i$ be associated with a cryptographically signed identity key $K_i$ issued upon contribution, and let all revisions be mapped as a directed acyclic graph $\mathcal{G}_A$ of state transformations:
\[
\mathcal{G}_A = \left(V = \{A_0, A_1, \dots, A_n\},\ E = \{(A_i, A_j)\ |\ A_j = \text{revise}(A_i)\} \right)
\]
Each edge $(A_i, A_j)$ is signed by the same identity key $K_i$, and every revision includes a semantic diff vector $\vec{\delta}_{ij}$ encoded as machine-readable logical and textual transformations. Retractions are not deletions but terminal nodes with an attached reason vector $\vec{r}_i$, signed and timestamped. These become audit trails, preserving the full epistemic evolution of the claim and the author’s response.

The inclusion of null results—where hypothesis $H$ is tested under methodology $M$ and no statistically or epistemically valid effect is detected—addresses a core asymmetry. Let $R_H = \emptyset$ denote a null result; in standard models, this remains unpublished. In the identity-linked model, such results are immutably committed to the ledger with meta-signatures confirming:
\[
\exists\, (M, D) \text{ such that } H \text{ evaluated on } D \Rightarrow R_H = \emptyset, \quad \text{logged as } \perp_H
\]
This negation is constructive: it bounds the epistemic domain of $H$ and calibrates posterior belief distribution in related claims. Repeated null results generate Bayesian dampening of related hypotheses’ priors. When identity is linked, these are not anonymous statistical ghosts, but scholarly signals reinforcing methodological transparency and scope.

Reputation, in this schema, becomes temporally weighted and topologically distributed. The notion of “sins of the past”—false claims, overextensions, or errors—is transformed into a vector of intellectual continuity. Identity continuity, enforced via cryptographic keys, ensures that individuals can demonstrate correction, engagement, and reflexive growth. Let the epistemic influence $\epsilon_t(K_i)$ of identity $K_i$ at time $t$ be computed as:
\[
\epsilon_t(K_i) = \sum_{j=1}^{n} w_j \cdot f_j(A_j, t)
\]
where $f_j$ is a valuation function over artefacts authored or corrected by $K_i$ and $w_j$ are weights based on downstream replication, community commentary, and contradiction resolution. A negative finding that prevents a thousand false affirmations has positive $\epsilon_t$, weighted by avoided cost.

This model necessitates persistent ethical identity: authors are not judged on static status but evolving contribution. Corrections are incentivised through reputation accrual, not punishment. Machine-readable provenance chains, public commentary logs, and immutable retraction signatures collectively produce a traceable, revisable knowledge structure.

By formalising epistemic correction as a constructive act, and anchoring all scholarly outputs—affirmative, negative, or negated—to enduring identity, we move from a brittle system of perceived infallibility to a robust topology of intellectual resilience. Knowledge becomes a revisable structure. Scholars become epistemic agents with accountable history. Errors become artefacts of value, and absence becomes a form of signal.

\subsection{Retractions as Integral Scholarly Outputs}

Retractions, in the existing scholarly system, are treated not as knowledge events but as reputational crises. The binary structure of publication—accept or reject—extends to the lifecycle of the paper itself: once published, a paper is presumed valid unless removed. Retractions are thus perceived as erasures rather than additions, negations rather than epistemic artefacts. This mischaracterisation stems from a model of scientific authorship that privileges finality and suppresses traceability.

We propose instead a model wherein retractions constitute integral outputs of scholarship, logged immutably, structured semantically, and linked to persistent identity. The epistemic structure must transition from a monolithic publication unit $P$ to a directed acyclic graph (DAG) of state-transformed nodes $\mathcal{R}_P = \{P_0, P_1, ..., P_n\}$, where each $P_i$ represents a published or revised instance of the work and $\text{retract}(P_i)$ results in a terminal node $R_i$:
\[
R_i = \text{retract}(P_i) = (P_i, \vec{\rho}_i, K_i, t_i),
\]
with $\vec{\rho}_i$ representing a structured reason vector (e.g.\ methodological flaw, ethical breach, data falsity), $K_i$ the persistent identity key of the author or editorial body, and $t_i$ a cryptographically timestamped ledger entry. Retractions are thus not deletions but extensions—epistemic outputs that signal the resolution of contested or invalidated claims.

This structure creates auditability. Unlike the present system where retractions are often hidden or buried, each $R_i$ node is discoverable, queryable, and semantically linked. For any citation graph $G_C$, the inclusion of a retracted work triggers propagation rules: a citation to $P_i$ post-$t_i$ is annotated with a verifiable retraction flag. This creates downstream epistemic hygiene. Machine-readable registries of retraction states enable AI systems, reviewers, and readers to assess the integrity of knowledge claims without requiring opaque editorial gatekeeping.

Moreover, authorial identity is central. Retractions linked to pseudonymous or transient identifiers erode epistemic accountability. In contrast, identity-bound retraction logs allow for longitudinal integrity assessments. A contributor’s scholarly credibility is computed not by the absence of error, but by the transparency of correction. Let the rectification score $\mathcal{S}_{\text{rect}}(K)$ for a given contributor $K$ be defined as:
\[
\mathcal{S}_{\text{rect}}(K) = \sum_{i=1}^{m} \gamma_i \cdot \mathbb{I}_{\text{voluntary}}(R_i),
\]
where each $R_i$ is a retraction initiated or accepted by $K$, $\gamma_i$ is the weighted impact of the correction (e.g.\ based on propagation, citations, contradiction resolution), and $\mathbb{I}_{\text{voluntary}}$ is an indicator function reflecting proactive engagement.

Through this architecture, retractions become trust signals. A system which normalises and formally integrates them as scholarly outputs enhances rather than diminishes the epistemic field. Papers become traceable entities with revision trajectories. Authors gain reputational capital not by evading error but by surfacing and resolving it. The field, in turn, benefits from structured doubt, documented contradiction, and an ontology that includes disconfirmation as generative knowledge.

\subsection{Null Results and the Epistemology of Absence}

The asymmetry of scholarly publication toward positive results generates an epistemically distorted landscape, wherein absence of effect is epistemologically penalised and methodologically silenced. This condition is not merely a sociological bias but a structural flaw in the ontology of accepted knowledge. In a rational scientific process, absence—when properly evidenced—is as informative as presence; null results delimit explanatory boundaries and prevent the overextension of claims. Yet under current regimes, such results are relegated to unpublished “file drawers,” excluded from citation networks, and epistemically orphaned.

We define a null result as a statistically or theoretically grounded outcome that affirms the insufficiency of a proposed causal or correlational relationship under specified experimental or inferential conditions. Formally, let a hypothesis $H$ be tested on dataset $D$ via methodology $M$, producing a result vector $\vec{r}$ with summary test statistic $\tau(H, D, M)$. When:
\[
\tau(H, D, M) \leq \alpha
\]
for a predefined effect threshold $\alpha$ (which may include adjusted Bayesian posterior, frequentist $p$-value, or information criteria score), and assuming model validity and data integrity, the result $\perp_H$ is logged as a structured null claim.

In an identity-linked, append-only infrastructure, such null claims are not discarded but permanently written to the epistemic ledger:
\[
\perp_H = (\text{null}, H, D, M, K_i, t),
\]
with $K_i$ the cryptographic key of the contributor and $t$ the timestamp of submission. Each null claim includes a machine-readable assertion ontology—defining the domain of disconfirmation—and semantic tags for citation and contradiction linkage. In this model, the absence of an effect becomes a first-class epistemic citizen, searchable, mappable, and incorporable into meta-analyses and AI-driven synthesis engines.

The epistemology of absence then becomes active rather than passive. Consider a hypothesis space $\mathcal{H}$ over domain $\mathcal{D}$; for any $H_i \in \mathcal{H}$, the accumulation of independent nulls $\{\perp_{H_i}^{(1)}, \dots, \perp_{H_i}^{(n)}\}$ over diverse datasets $D_j$ acts as a probabilistic damping function on future belief:
\[
\pi_{t+1}(H_i) = \lambda \cdot \pi_t(H_i), \quad \lambda < 1
\]
where $\pi$ is a belief or funding-weight prior. Null results thus perform negative feedback across the scientific corpus, modulating epistemic attention and resource allocation.

Critically, null publication also supports replication culture. By exposing voids, it enables targeted testing, theory refinement, and cumulative disconfirmation. AI-based review systems can index null structures to detect overfitting, publication inflation, and hypothesis redundancy.

From an ethical standpoint, failure to publish null results constitutes an epistemic injustice: it buries contributions under the pretence of non-contribution. When identity is cryptographically bound, and negative findings are preserved, each instance of well-structured absence becomes a durable signal of scientific restraint, methodological clarity, and conceptual refinement. 

The scholarly record thus shifts from a biased portrait of validated hypotheses to a map of epistemic contour—what is true, what is false, and what has been found wanting. The ledger becomes a cartography of knowledge and absence, equally valued and permanently traceable.

\subsection{Historical Claims and the Reputation Gradient}

In the prevailing academic regime, knowledge claims ossify into historical artefacts without structured continuity. Once published, a claim becomes temporally fixed and often decoupled from subsequent corrections, reinterpretations, or falsifications. This disjuncture results in the persistence of epistemically outdated or incorrect assertions, especially when high-prestige publications are cited uncritically across generations. The absence of formal temporal decay functions or revision-linked credentialing mechanisms creates an epistemic inertia: a claim’s age and citation volume are conflated with its truth value.

To address this, we define a reputation gradient $\nabla_\mathcal{R}(H, t)$ over a hypothesis $H$ as the derivative of credibility over time, weighted by the integrity of authorial correction, replication consistency, and downstream epistemic effect. Let:
\[
\nabla_\mathcal{R}(H, t) = \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n} \phi_i \cdot \kappa_i(H, t) \right),
\]
where each $\kappa_i$ is the credibility kernel derived from a citation, replication, or contradiction event, and $\phi_i$ represents the weight conferred by identity persistence and correction traceability.

This gradient is not computed merely from numerical citation counts but from the structural properties of the knowledge graph: whether citations are affirmative, critical, or contradictory; whether the author engaged in corrections; whether later developments rendered the claim obsolete. Identity-linked publishing ensures that these corrections are not anonymous erasures but public, cryptographically signed engagements. As such, a historical claim is not an inert citation object but an evolving node within an epistemic DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) of knowledge transformation.

Let each knowledge unit $K$ authored at time $t_0$ evolve through revisions, critiques, and replications $\{K_1, \dots, K_n\}$. Each transition $(K_i \to K_{i+1})$ is identity-signed and ledger-recorded. The cumulative integrity of an author over a body of claims is not a static h-index but a weighted traceable path $\mathcal{T}(K_i)$ through the epistemic DAG:
\[
\mathcal{T}(K_i) = \sum_{j=1}^{m} \omega_j \cdot \mathbb{I}_{\text{engage}}(K_j),
\]
where $\omega_j$ represents the impact factor of each node, and $\mathbb{I}_{\text{engage}}$ is an indicator of authorial involvement in post-publication discourse—commentary, correction, or contextualisation.

Historical epistemology thereby becomes formalised and computable. Claims are not immune to temporal audit but subject to long-memory decay and credibility reweighting. AI agents can parse these graphs to prioritise recent, corrected, and debated nodes over legacy claims frozen by editorial prestige. The effect is a flattening of epistemic hierarchy: truth value becomes a dynamic output of the network, not a static property of early publication.

Crucially, the social function of the scholar transforms under this system. Reputation is not a front-loaded capital spent through publication but a gradient accrued through sustained epistemic accountability. Historical claims become reputational vectors, and their maintenance or amendment is visible to all future systems of evaluation.

The result is not merely a more accurate corpus of knowledge, but a new scholarly ethos—where identity, correction, and epistemic time are linked, weighted, and transparent across the lifespan of every contribution.

\subsection{Identity, Continuity, and the Ethics of Correction}

The ethical legitimacy of the scholarly enterprise rests upon the continuity of responsibility—one cannot claim epistemic credit without bearing epistemic burden. In the current system, however, identity is fragmented and often dissociated from post-publication correction. Authors may move institutions, change identifiers, or publish under disjointed credentials. Retractions and errors, if acknowledged at all, are frequently buried in metadata or issued anonymously. The result is an epistemic system that rewards publication but obscures responsibility.

In a computational framework where scholarly contributions are persistently identity-bound—via cryptographic signatures, institutional proofs, or reputation-linked tokens—correction becomes not a discretionary act but an ethical function embedded in the publishing protocol. Let each published unit $P_i$ be associated with an immutable identity key $K_i \in \mathcal{K}$ and a correction vector $\vec{C}_i(t)$, denoting changes, retractions, or contextual amendments issued over time $t$:
\[
\vec{C}_i(t) = \left\{ c_j \mid c_j = (\delta_j, \tau_j, \sigma_j), \, j \in \mathbb{N} \right\},
\]
where $\delta_j$ is the type of correction (e.g.\ factual error, retraction, reinterpretation), $\tau_j$ the timestamp, and $\sigma_j$ the signature of $K_i$. The corpus of scholarly work becomes not a static archive but a morphogenic structure—a record of corrections, interpretations, and re-commitments, all signed and linked to accountable agents.

This model instantiates an ethics of correction grounded in continuity: authors are incentivised to remain tethered to their claims, revisiting, updating, or rebutting them as new information arises. The value of $\mathcal{S}_{\text{ethics}}(K)$, a scholarly ethical integrity score, can be defined as:
\[
\mathcal{S}_{\text{ethics}}(K) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left[ \beta_i \cdot \mathbb{I}_{\text{proactive}}(\vec{C}_i) + \gamma_i \cdot \mathbb{I}_{\text{responsive}}(\vec{C}_i) \right],
\]
where $\beta_i$ and $\gamma_i$ are impact-based weightings and $\mathbb{I}_{\text{proactive}}$ or $\mathbb{I}_{\text{responsive}}$ indicate whether the correction was initiated voluntarily or in response to external challenge. This computation is AI-verifiable, ledger-queryable, and institutionally referenceable.

Identity continuity also enables longitudinal analysis of scholarly character. A system that tracks epistemic amendments over time produces not only paper metrics but contributor epistemologies. AI agents can generate evolution maps for each author, visualising conceptual refinement, correction responsiveness, and error rates. These become not tools of judgment, but dimensions of trust.

Crucially, such transparency mitigates the punitive stigma attached to correction. In a system where accountability is expected and normalised, the issuance of a correction enhances rather than depletes reputation. It signals engagement, self-awareness, and epistemic maturity. This contrasts sharply with the current structure, where avoidance of correction is often a rational, if unethical, choice.

The ethical imperative, then, is structural: only a system that enforces identity continuity can produce a culture of epistemic correction. Fragmented identity produces fragmented responsibility. Cryptographically persistent identifiers, linked to scholarly outputs across time and domain, are necessary—not as surveillance but as the minimal infrastructure for moral scientific authorship.

Through such design, correction ceases to be an act of shame. It becomes an index of virtue—measurable, traceable, and rewarded.

\subsection{From Negative Findings to Constructive Knowledge Structures}

Negative findings—results that fail to support a given hypothesis—constitute a fundamental epistemic component of scientific reasoning, yet remain systematically underrepresented in the published literature. This asymmetry introduces publication bias, distorts meta-analyses, and promotes an illusory inflation of effect sizes. The issue, extensively reviewed in Dwan et al.\ (2013), reveals that over 60\% of clinical trials with null results remain unpublished, contributing to what is known as the "file drawer problem" \cite{dwan2013systematic}. The epistemological consequence is that absence of effect is misread as absence of inquiry.

In a formal schema, let $\mathcal{H}$ be a hypothesis space over empirical domain $\mathcal{D}$. For any null outcome $\perp_{H_i}$ (where $H_i \in \mathcal{H}$), failure to publish translates into a deletion of the node from the knowledge graph $\mathcal{G}_K$:
\[
\mathcal{G}_K := \{\text{claims}, \text{data}, \text{replications}, \text{refutations}\},
\]
with $\mathcal{G}_K \setminus \perp_{H_i}$ representing a structurally compromised inferential space.

By contrast, a ledgered system where negative results are identity-linked and hash-anchored becomes capable of supporting an enriched epistemic topology. Null results, when properly encoded and semantically tagged, serve as demarcation lines—circumscribing what does not work, under what conditions, and with what boundary constraints. Fanelli (2012) argues that null results reduce the propagation of false positives and act as epistemic stabilisers in rapidly iterating fields \cite{fanelli2012negative}.

To integrate such findings constructively, a metadata schema must support structured semantic roles for nulls. Let:
\[
N := (\text{Domain}, \text{Method}, \text{Effect Size}, \text{Confidence}, \text{Signatory}),
\]
be a canonical form for any published null. Within a blockchain-based infrastructure, $N$ is cryptographically signed and stored as an immutable append-only ledger entry. This permits downstream AI agents and human scholars to retrieve and evaluate failure conditions with equal visibility as positive claims.

Moreover, a revised citation model must include negative findings in the impact computation. Let the impact weight $w_i$ for a contribution be:
\[
w_i = \alpha C_i^+ + \beta C_i^-,
\]
where $C_i^+$ and $C_i^-$ are counts of positive and null citations, respectively, and $\beta \geq \alpha > 0$ under contexts valuing caution and boundary demarcation. Such weighting schemes have been proposed in frameworks like the Evidence-Based Research paradigm, promoting cumulative rather than purely affirmative science \cite{glasziou2014reducing}.

The constructive integration of null results also enhances reproducibility. When negative findings are logged, reproduced, and linked across trials, AI-based synthesis tools can infer latent structure, detect methodological weaknesses, and cluster boundary conditions across disciplines. This networked accumulation of what does not work becomes the scaffolding on which robust, generalisable findings are built.

Finally, identity persistence ensures that such contributions are reputationally recognised. The stigma of null publication is reduced when such results are indexed, cited, and computationally visible. Scholars are no longer punished for falsifiability or caution, but are structurally integrated into the epistemic record—visible not only as discoverers, but as delimiters.





\section{Reputation Systems and Identity Verification}

The integrity of scholarly ecosystems rests not only on the validity of individual claims, but on the trustworthiness of the actors who assert them. In traditional models, publication itself is weighted more heavily than authorial credibility over time, and peer review remains predominantly anonymous—limiting both transparency and deterrence for bad-faith engagement. A reformed system must reconstruct authorship, commentary, and editorial roles as identity-linked operations, cryptographically verifiable and reputationally cumulative. 

Let $\mathcal{A}$ be the set of all agents within a publication system, and each $a \in \mathcal{A}$ is associated with a persistent, verifiable identity $I_a$ such that:
\[
I_a := \langle \text{institutional\_credential}, \text{digital\_signature}, \text{public\_ledger\_key} \rangle
\]
This identity is not ephemeral nor optional—it is recorded immutably and tied to every publication, review, commentary, and response action conducted within the scholarly ledger $\mathcal{L}$. This binding makes it possible to trace contribution trees and accountability vectors over time, including corrections, errors, and commentary.

A key epistemic shift emerges: every contribution becomes an attributed vector in the reputation space $\mathbb{R}^n$, where trust is not binary (trusted/untrusted) but accumulated through interactive engagement. The reputation function $\mathcal{R} : \mathcal{A} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ evolves through metrics including:
\[
\mathcal{R}(a) = \alpha \cdot \text{validated\_claims}_a + \beta \cdot \text{endorsed\_reviews}_a + \gamma \cdot \text{retracted\_corrections}_a^{-1}
\]
with tunable weights $\alpha, \beta, \gamma \in \mathbb{R}^+$, and where higher $\mathcal{R}$ scores correlate with greater scholarly influence and system privileges (e.g.\ review authority, accelerated publication windows, etc.).

Unlike opaque editorial hierarchies, this model builds a fully transparent ledger of academic behaviour. Agents who consistently provide accurate reviews and correct their own past claims improve their influence. Conversely, those identified as generating false reviews, performing in bad faith, or abusing commentary privileges accumulate penalties—visible to all other agents. Reputation decays over time if contributions lapse, incentivising continual epistemic engagement.

Identity-linked systems also enable cross-domain verification. A reviewer in computational linguistics who critiques a paper in economics must possess credentials in both or demonstrate historically valid cross-field review capacity. This validation is enforced through identity-credential graphs, where each edge represents a validated publication or cross-disciplinary endorsement.

This section establishes the need for a cryptographically anchored, transparent reputation system that restores trust in the scholarly process. Rather than relying on journals to curate prestige, we compute and display epistemic behaviour directly. Every actor becomes a traceable participant. Every correction a mark of integrity. Every citation a reputational signal.

Only such a system can disincentivise trolling, reward meaningful contribution, and elevate peer review from hidden moderation to formal scholarly authorship.

\subsection{Disincentivising Trolling through Public Identity}

Anonymity in academic peer review was originally implemented to promote unbiased evaluation and shield reviewers from retaliation, but contemporary behavioural research demonstrates its strong correlation with increased antisocial behaviour~\cite{cheng2017anyone}. Empirical analysis shows that anonymity reduces reputational consequences and enables low-effort or hostile discourse~\cite{cheng2017anyone}. \cite{cheng2017anyone} quantify how users previously exhibiting civil conduct engage in trolling once anonymous, confirming anonymity as a behavioural catalyst.

\cite{bernstein20114chan} highlight how platforms structured around anonymity, such as 4chan, facilitate incivility and disinhibition, evidencing the degradation of discourse in identity-free environments. The link between stable identity and constructive participation suggests that persistence of identity is epistemically valuable, not merely socially desirable~\cite{bernstein20114chan}. Anonymity thus introduces systemic epistemic risk by disincentivising accountability~\cite{bernstein20114chan}.

The introduction of verifiable identity frameworks—such as cryptographically signed credentials linked to immutable publication records—shifts the review structure toward one where every comment contributes to an individual's public intellectual footprint~\cite{goldman1999knowledge}. This traceability reorients the incentive landscape, reinforcing norms of substantive critique and suppressing performative or malicious behaviour~\cite{hardin1993epistemic}. As a result, trolling is not simply discouraged; it becomes reputationally self-defeating~\cite{goldman1999knowledge}.

\subsection{Reviewers as Intellectual Contributors}

The contribution of peer reviewers has traditionally been viewed as ancillary, yet analytical decomposition of the review process reveals significant intellectual labour underpinning the development of scholarly quality~\cite{smith2006peer}. Reviewers engage in reconstructive critique, error correction, and epistemic framing, actions which constitute co-authorship in all but name~\cite{godlee2002making}. This uncredited labour perpetuates epistemological asymmetries and contributes to burnout in expert communities~\cite{smith2006peer}.

Recent proposals have advanced systems whereby reviewers receive citable recognition for their evaluative inputs~\cite{rosshell1998peer}, allowing their contribution to be formally integrated into the intellectual trace of a publication. This model treats reviewing as a form of constructive authorship, measured not by original hypothesis but by dialectical engagement and refinement~\cite{rosshell1998peer}. Such shifts align with virtue epistemology in which knowledge acquisition is a distributed cooperative process rather than individualistic derivation~\cite{zollman2007network}.

Blockchain-supported commentary layers allow reviewer interventions to persist as part of the epistemic record, giving provenance to arguments and contextualising revisions~\cite{powell2016too}. This technical infrastructure supports a reconceptualisation of the reviewer’s role, transforming them from invisible gatekeepers into cited interlocutors~\cite{godlee2002making}.

\subsection{Subcitation Networks and Commentary Histories}

Subcitation networks—chains of dependent, non-primary references that trace influence and methodological inheritance—constitute a latent but critical component of scholarly infrastructure~\cite{de_solla_price1965networks}. These subcited elements, including figures, methods, and data pipelines, are often omitted from direct citation but persist as epistemic scaffolding across domains~\cite{latour1987science}. By explicitly mapping these interstitial connections, commentary histories can be reconstructed to reveal the true intellectual topology of a field~\cite{bornmann2008citation}.

This expanded notion of citation lineage aligns with contemporary work in epistemic network theory, where the value of a contribution is modelled not solely through citation count but via positional centrality in knowledge graphs~\cite{zhu2020citation}. Commentary histories, preserved as versioned, timestamped overlays, allow researchers to trace interpretative transformations and contestations through a paper’s lifecycle, yielding transparency and diachronic clarity~\cite{fortunato2018science}.

When combined with blockchain-based immutability, these histories acquire both referential fixity and verifiability, protecting the continuity of scholarly dialogue from erasure or revisionism~\cite{tilly2022blockchain}. The shift from static citation to dynamic commentary networks enables formal recognition of intellectual influence beyond authorship, constructing a weighted ecology of contribution~\cite{newman2018networks}.




\section{Evaluation Metrics in the Commentary Paradigm}

The evaluation of scholarly work must evolve in tandem with the architecture of publication and commentary. Under a commentary-based paradigm, where review is continuous, publicly linked, and identity-bound, the traditional metrics—impact factor, h-index, raw citation counts—are insufficient and often misleading. These aggregate values fail to distinguish between intellectual use, methodological inheritance, citation inflation, or critical repudiation. A reformed evaluation schema requires formal metrics that reflect the epistemic structure, replicative influence, and corrective contribution of each work and its authorial network.

Let $\mathcal{P}$ be the set of all published artefacts (articles, reviews, corrections, commentaries), and $\mathcal{C}_p$ be the set of all commentaries linked to a given publication $p \in \mathcal{P}$. Each commentary $c \in \mathcal{C}_p$ can be weighted by its type: validation, contradiction, extension, replication, or error identification. Formally, define the commentary vector for $p$:
\[
\mathbf{v}_p = \langle v, x, r, e \rangle
\]
where $v$ is the count of validated endorsements, $x$ extensions or derivatives, $r$ replication confirmations, and $e$ identified errors. A paper’s evaluation metric becomes a multivariate function over $\mathbf{v}_p$, prioritising epistemic contribution over raw volume.

Furthermore, this paradigm shifts attention to secondary influence and use-context propagation. A single study's methods reused across domains may offer more value than one amassing generic citations. Therefore, commentary is not merely reaction but structural linkage, and it is tracked in the form of subcitation graphs—recursive links of commentary on commentaries, derivations from derivations, and method adoption trees. Each node in the scholarly graph is evaluated both for origin contribution and generative capacity.

AI agents continuously compute these metrics in real time across a distributed citation ledger. They assess novelty, replication support, semantic divergence, and commentary integrity. Error identification is elevated, not punished. The scholarly ecosystem is reshaped from static recognition to dynamic structural participation—where each interaction deepens the epistemic field, and every contribution is traced, weighted, and formally embedded in the reputational and evaluative fabric.

\subsection{Subcitation Density and Derivative Use}

Subcitation density—the frequency with which indirect or derivative elements of a work are reused across subsequent literature—serves as an epistemological metric of latent influence~\cite{small1973co}. Unlike traditional citation counts, which track explicit acknowledgements, subcitation density measures the propagation of concepts, methods, or structures that are reused without direct attribution, capturing the invisible scaffolding of research~\cite{perkins2014evaluating}. This form of latent citation reflects not merely visibility, but integration, thereby aligning more closely with Kuhnian notions of normal science~\cite{kuhn1962structure}.

Quantitative models employing bibliographic coupling and co-citation analysis allow reconstruction of these derivative relationships~\cite{boyack2005mapping}. When overlaid onto temporally indexed citation graphs, they reveal chains of conceptual lineage and cross-field migration of techniques, indicating interdisciplinary uptake and methodological convergence~\cite{glanzel2004towards}. A paper with high subcitation density may thus underpin theoretical or practical paradigms even when bypassed in formal bibliographies~\cite{bornmann2011usefulness}.

By encoding these connections within append-only ledgers or immutable databases, a full trace of derivative scholarly use can be made auditable and resistant to obfuscation~\cite{tilly2022blockchain}. Integrating such metrics into scholarly evaluation provides a more nuanced and just framework for assessing impact, where quiet foundational work receives epistemic recognition proportional to its structural role~\cite{waltman2016review}.

\subsection{Replication Quotient and Method Adoption}

The replication quotient of a scholarly work—defined as the ratio of independent replications to total citations—offers a metric for empirical robustness and scientific reliability~\cite{ioannidis2005most}. In fields where replicability is a cornerstone of validity, this quotient captures the extent to which a study's findings withstand empirical scrutiny, distinguishing transient claims from foundational contributions~\cite{open2015estimating}. Low replication quotients, especially when coupled with high citation counts, may indicate trend-driven diffusion rather than epistemic reliability~\cite{fanelli2018meta}.

Method adoption further complements this measure by tracing the diffusion of protocols, analytical pipelines, and experimental frameworks independent of a study’s results~\cite{moravcsik1975some}. High adoption of methods—even in the absence of confirmatory results—signals technical utility and structural influence. For instance, a statistical approach may be cited across numerous domains without direct validation of its originating hypotheses, functioning as an epistemic toolset rather than a propositional claim~\cite{greenland2016statistical}.

Mapping these phenomena requires machine-readable metadata embedded within scholarly registries, permitting aggregation of replication attempts, failure reports, and method reuse under unified author identifiers~\cite{hardwicke2020data}. Combined with identity-linked provenance ledgers, this allows both positive and negative validations to be registered immutably, correcting current publication biases against null or failed replications~\cite{munafò2017manifesto}. Such a system reorients scholarly value toward verifiability and constructive technical inheritance.


\subsection{Error Discovery as Intellectual Contribution}

The identification of errors in published literature is not merely a corrective gesture but an epistemically generative act. In falsificationist science, as advocated by Popper~\cite{popper2005logic}, progress is measured by the systematic exposure of flaws in prevailing hypotheses. Consequently, detecting and formally articulating errors—whether methodological, logical, or interpretive—constitutes a legitimate scholarly contribution that refines the knowledge base and elevates evidentiary standards~\cite{lakatos1978philosophical}.

Traditionally, such corrective acts have been undervalued or discouraged by prestige journals, creating disincentives to challenge consensus or dominant figures. Yet error detection fulfills the same function as positive contribution in Karl Popper’s demarcation criterion: falsifiability and rigorous testing define science, not acquiescence~\cite{haack2003defending}. Within a digital infrastructure that registers commentary as a first-class object and links contributions to verified identity, error detection becomes publicly acknowledged and preserved—no longer relegated to the footnotes of correction letters~\cite{baro2020tracking}.

Moreover, tracking the frequency, type, and origin of identified errors across time yields diagnostic metrics for epistemic health and systemic bias~\cite{altmejd2019predictable}. For example, if Bayesian p-hacking is frequently uncovered in a particular field or journal, that fact itself is a scholarly observation. Hence, repositories such as PubPeer and projects like the Open Research Knowledge Graph (ORKG) are early attempts to formalise the integration of error discovery into scholarly metadata~\cite{jaradeh2019open}. Formalising these acts within identity-linked public commentary would normalise vigilance as a scholarly virtue.





\section{Governance, Incentives, and Normative Implications}

The legitimacy of any scholarly communication system depends not only on its structural affordances—such as transparency, traceability, and replicability—but also on the institutional and normative scaffolding that governs its operations. In a decentralised, commentary-driven model of academic exchange, governance cannot be monolithic or editorially opaque. It must instead emerge from formalised incentive structures, identity-linked engagement, and algorithmically transparent rulesets designed for systemic trust.

Let $\mathcal{G} = \{r_1, r_2, ..., r_n\}$ represent the set of governance rules, where each $r_i$ is an enforceable norm bound to a smart contract instance on a publicly auditable ledger. These rules define permissible actions—submitting commentary, issuing retractions, modifying reputational weightings—within the scholarly platform $\mathcal{S}$. Actions are subject to community validation through $\mathcal{V}: \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{G} \rightarrow \{0,1\}$, where $\mathcal{A}$ is the agent set and $\mathcal{V}$ encodes consensus legitimacy.

Incentives are structured to reward not just publication, but epistemically valuable behaviours—verifying results, uncovering methodological flaws, issuing corrigenda, and contributing substantive commentary. Each such act is assigned a trust delta $\Delta T_a \in \mathbb{R}$ for agent $a$, such that their cumulative influence evolves via:

\[
T_{a}^{t+1} = T_{a}^{t} + \Delta T_a = T_{a}^{t} + \lambda_1 \cdot \text{endorsements}_a - \lambda_2 \cdot \text{flagged\_errors}_a + \lambda_3 \cdot \text{replication\_support}_a
\]

with $\lambda_i$ defining system-level weightings approved through governance protocol $\mathcal{G}$. The aim is not to punish dissent or experimentation, but to align structural incentives with the long-term goals of epistemic robustness and cumulative public knowledge.

This model also integrates the notion of \textbf{public intellectual labour}. Review, commentary, and critical engagement are no longer informal or unpaid obligations; they are logged contributions, each tied to a verified identity, and subject to formal recognition, traceable attribution, and even economic reward via tokenised value systems. Smart contracts allocate resource streams to top-ranked reviewers or replicators, bypassing the opaque reward mechanisms of journal hierarchies.

Normatively, the integration of identity verification systems addresses the ethical need for accountability and the legal requirement for data provenance. It curtails trolling, bad-faith commentary, and falsified engagement by binding all epistemic actions to a verified, continuous identity. Reputation becomes cumulative, persistent, and visible—capable of decay, redemption, and reinforcement.

This infrastructure decentralises authority while formalising responsibility. The resulting scholarly ecosystem is not only more dynamic and transparent—it is structurally aligned to reward intellectual integrity, foster methodological rigour, and accelerate the self-corrective dynamics of science and scholarship.

\subsection{Transparent Weighting and Review Quality Scoring}

Transparent weighting of scholarly reviews allows the differentiation between perfunctory comment and substantive critical engagement. Current systems treat peer review as a binary gatekeeping function, omitting fine-grained analysis of review quality~\cite{mulligan2013peer}. However, by scoring reviews based on criteria such as methodological scrutiny, citation contextualisation, and falsification attempt, one can algorithmically assess the intellectual contribution of each reviewer~\cite{bornmann2011scientific}. These metrics must be public, linked to reviewer identity, and recorded on tamper-proof infrastructure to incentivise rigour over favouritism.

Using a weighted scoring model based on latent semantic analysis of review text and cross-referenced against citation and replication outcomes, one can derive a composite “review quality index” (RQI)~\cite{rowhani2014quantifying}. The RQI would incorporate ex post outcomes such as subsequent corrections, citations of identified errors, and predictive accuracy of critique. This moves review from a subjective appraisal to a citable contribution, functionally equivalent to publication, and shifts norms from gatekeeping toward constructive knowledge participation~\cite{teixeira2020role}.

Further, integrating these scores into reviewer profiles over time—especially when linked via verified identity systems—enables longitudinal trust mapping, allowing institutions to select reviewers based not on status or proximity, but evidentiary track record. This epistemic memory, once stored in immutable form, aligns incentives and raises standards through reputational investment~\cite{tennant2017state}.


\subsection{Open Scholarship and Intellectual Labour}

The production of knowledge is a cumulative and distributed labour process. Under closed publication models, intellectual contributions such as peer review, commentary, or critical replication are systematically devalued, even though they contribute to epistemic progress~\cite{buranyi2017sick}. The neoliberal commodification of publication—driven by prestige hierarchies and revenue-generating journals—obscures the collaborative labour that underpins science and scholarship~\cite{edwards2016open}. Open scholarship seeks to rectify this by recognising all forms of scholarly input as first-class outputs, attributing labour transparently and immutably.

Identity-linked systems integrated with blockchain infrastructure can record and timestamp each act of knowledge production—from review to data cleaning—ensuring verifiable provenance and enabling citation-level credit assignment~\cite{sholler2019enabling}. This transforms scholarship from an artefact-centric to a process-centric model, valuing the pathways through which knowledge is stabilised. Just as code repositories track each commit, scholarly infrastructure must track and attribute each epistemic intervention~\cite{boulton2012science}.

Furthermore, the ethics of labour recognition demand that invisible work—frequently gendered, racialised, and institutionally suppressed—be brought into epistemic visibility~\cite{crane2022invisible}. Repositories such as ORCID and the CRediT taxonomy lay the groundwork for granular attribution, but must be extended to incorporate all layers of academic contribution within a linked identity framework. Recognition, credit, and citation must be accorded not merely to final authorship, but to all steps of scholarly construction and critical contestation~\cite{mongeon2016coauthorship}.


\subsection{Ethical and Legal Considerations}

The transformation of peer review into a public, identity-linked, and immutable process raises significant ethical and legal implications. The linkage of commentary, review, and revision histories to verified identities introduces new dimensions of accountability, but also heightens privacy risks, especially where controversial or politically sensitive subjects are involved~\cite{resnik2010ethics}. Legal frameworks surrounding data protection, such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), require that systems for scholarly identity management maintain strict compliance, including provisions for erasure, consent, and proportional exposure~\cite{voigt2017eu}.

Moreover, the attribution of intellectual labour in review must navigate questions of consent and ownership. Reviews recorded in immutable ledgers may fall under intellectual property regimes, necessitating that reviewers have explicit rights over how their assessments are stored and reused~\cite{groshek2017ownership}. Public commentary as scholarship also implicates defamation law, especially in jurisdictions with weaker protections for academic critique. The integrity of a system based on open identity must balance transparency with protections against reputational harm, harassment, and misattribution~\cite{blaszczynski2019safeguarding}.

At the same time, anonymity has historically protected whistleblowers and dissenting voices in scientific communities. Removing such protections wholesale may suppress necessary but unpopular interventions. A robust ethical architecture must distinguish between transparency for accountability and pseudonymity for safety, using cryptographic proofs to mediate claims without compromising individuals~\cite{shen2022anonymous}. Ultimately, the challenge lies in embedding legal safeguards, ethical review, and dynamic consent into the technological fabric of knowledge infrastructure~\cite{bechmann2021responsible}.




\section{Epistemology, Education, and the Philosophy of Citation}

The architecture of scholarly communication is a reflection of our collective epistemology—our theory of knowledge, its formation, validation, and transmission. Citation is not merely a referential tool; it is an epistemic act that encodes power, lineage, and legitimacy. Education, likewise, is not a neutral pipeline but a recursive system of knowledge curation, where what is taught reflects what has been cited, and what is cited reflects what is institutionally enshrined.

In classical epistemology, knowledge is often defined as “justified true belief,” yet in practice, justification is socially mediated. The citation economy governs this mediation, determining what counts as knowledge through recursive endorsement. This recursion is not a flaw but a structural feature—one that requires transparent instrumentation. Each citation embeds a directional link in the graph of epistemic authority, where repetition accrues legitimacy, and absence invites oblivion.

Educational structures reinforce this hierarchy. Curricula are constructed not from first principles, but from citational consensus. The process of disciplinary canon formation depends on an exclusionary logic, often eliminating unorthodox, negative, or paradigm-challenging results. The epistemic injustice this produces is not random; it is systematic. Only by tying citations to identity, commentary to permanence, and peer engagement to transparent traceability can the structure of knowledge be rebalanced.

In a blockchain-based system, each act of citation is fixed, verifiable, and temporally ordered. The recursive structure becomes legible. Identity ensures that credit and responsibility are inextricably linked, and the role of pedagogy shifts from transmission to traceability. What was once invisible—who cited whom, in what context, and with what philosophical allegiance—becomes part of the epistemic substrate. In such a system, knowledge ceases to be an archive of consensus and becomes instead a continuously unfolding structure of interpretable, contested, and annotated intellectual history.


\subsection{Citation as Memory: Epistemic Inheritance and Canon Formation}

Citation operates as the formal memory of scholarship, encoding trajectories of thought, genealogies of argument, and epistemic hierarchies~\cite{cronin1984citation}. Each citation links a present claim to its intellectual ancestry, constructing a lineage through which ideas gain legitimacy, authority, and institutional weight~\cite{garfield2006history}. This process forms canons—not through objective appraisal, but through social replication, institutional embedding, and strategic omission~\cite{martin2013genealogy}.

As Kuhn argues, paradigms are not merely frameworks for inquiry; they are sociocognitive structures reproduced through citational practice~\cite{kuhn1970structure}. When certain works are routinely cited as foundational while others are excluded or forgotten, a disciplinary memory is produced that privileges coherence over contestation. This is not neutral. Citation practice is political, shaping what knowledge is treated as central, peripheral, or nonexistent~\cite{harding1991whats}.

To render this memory epistemically transparent, we must link citations not only to documents, but to authorial identity, institutional context, and subsequent reception. Immutable infrastructures—anchored via blockchain—can trace citational flows, reveal omissions, and expose distortions in scholarly memory formation~\cite{davidson2018blockchain}. In doing so, they make possible a new meta-level of scholarly critique: one that does not merely track citations, but interrogates them as artefacts of power, memory, and exclusion~\cite{patel2022epistemic}.


\subsection{Paradigm Enforcement and Anomaly Suppression}

Within Kuhn’s model of scientific development, paradigms function not merely as explanatory frameworks but as normative regimes that define what constitutes a valid question, method, and result~\cite{kuhn1970structure}. This regulatory role extends to the peer review and citation practices that act as filters, rewarding paradigm-conforming work while suppressing anomalies that challenge foundational assumptions~\cite{longino1990science}. Anomalous findings—those that fail to align with prevailing theory—are frequently disregarded, under-reported, or rejected not on methodological grounds, but due to perceived epistemic illegitimacy~\cite{feyerabend1975against}.

Such suppression is not incidental; it is structural. In a competitive funding and publication economy, scholars are incentivised to reproduce canonical forms rather than explore epistemically disruptive alternatives~\cite{fanelli2012negative}. Reviewers—steeped in the dominant paradigm—act as gatekeepers who often mistake methodological heterodoxy for error, effectively enforcing a regime of intellectual orthodoxy~\cite{hess1997science}. This creates an illusion of theoretical consensus, artificially stabilising scientific fields and delaying necessary shifts.

Digital systems anchored in transparency and traceability can resist this suppression. Immutable commentary chains, public peer annotations, and version-controlled review histories allow anomalies to persist within the visible scholarly record~\cite{graham2014knowledge}. Such visibility transforms anomalies from private dismissals to public provocations, creating the epistemic conditions for paradigm critique and transformation. In a transparent ecosystem, the anomaly is no longer silenced; it becomes archived, interrogated, and potentially catalytic.


\subsection{Pedagogical Structures and Knowledge Stratification}

Pedagogical systems often operate as stratified conduits of epistemic authority, wherein content is curated, structured, and transmitted in accordance with prevailing paradigms~\cite{freire1970pedagogy}. Canonical texts, required readings, and lecture formats do not merely convey information; they encode epistemic hierarchies, reinforcing the legitimacy of certain authors, disciplines, and methodologies while marginalising dissenting perspectives~\cite{bourdieu1990reproduction}. The structuring of curricula thus plays a central role in entrenching dominant frameworks, often under the guise of neutrality or objectivity~\cite{apple1993official}.

This stratification has the effect of reproducing not only knowledge but its exclusions. As students ascend the academic hierarchy, they are trained not only in content but in citational etiquette, disciplinary allegiances, and the rhetorical codes of intellectual legitimacy~\cite{becher2001academic}. This process fosters intellectual conformity, as divergence from established trajectories is penalised through assessment systems, peer review, and career progression metrics~\cite{barnett1997higher}. In effect, education becomes a gatekeeping mechanism that filters novelty and instils paradigm adherence through formal instruction.

Transparent, decentralised commentary systems could destabilise this hierarchy. Open annotation of educational materials, visible citation flows, and commentary-linked identity structures enable pedagogical materials to be critiqued, recontextualised, and recomposed in real time~\cite{knight2014learning}. Such models resist ossification by allowing knowledge to evolve publicly and dialogically, challenging the notion of fixed curricula and enabling epistemic plurality within educational ecosystems~\cite{tseng2020blockchainpedagogy}.


\subsection{Citation as Dialogical Act: From Freire to Blockchain}

In Paulo Freire’s dialogical model of education, knowledge emerges through interaction, reflection, and co-authorship, rather than hierarchical transmission~\cite{freire1970pedagogy}. Citation, within this framework, is not merely an attributional tool but a dialogical gesture—a recognition of prior voices in an ongoing, collective epistemic conversation. This conceptualisation reframes citation as active participation in discourse, rather than passive compliance with scholarly formality~\cite{hooks1994teaching}.

Yet, the prevailing citation systems institutionalise authority, often reinforcing epistemic silos and hierarchies~\cite{latour1987science}. Rather than enabling dialogic exchange, citations function as gatekeeping mechanisms: they determine intellectual legitimacy, access to publication venues, and academic capital~\cite{merton1968matthew}. This leads to an entrenched canon and citational inertia, where only dominant voices are heard and deviations are epistemically marginalised~\cite{nash2019inequality}.

Blockchain-integrated citation systems reconfigure this dynamic. By enabling transparent, timestamped, identity-linked citation trails, these systems enact a Freirean ethic of visibility, dialogue, and accountability~\cite{roberts2019blockchainedu}. They support participatory citation ecosystems where contributions can be traced, updated, and critiqued publicly. In this reimagined dialogical space, citation regains its role as a site of epistemic interaction, not only recording scholarly dependencies but inviting response, reinterpretation, and constructive contestation~\cite{tseng2020blockchainpedagogy}.


\subsection{Identity and the Ethics of Acknowledgement}

Acknowledgement in scholarly discourse is not merely a formal gesture—it is an ethical act that recognises the origin of ideas, the labour of intellectual production, and the situated identity of the contributor~\cite{fricker2007epistemic}. Traditional citation practices, however, often efface the personal and contextual dimensions of knowledge-making. When citations become detached from identity, both the responsibility for errors and the credit for insight are obfuscated, leading to what Miranda Fricker terms “epistemic injustice”~\cite{fricker2007epistemic}.

The ethics of acknowledgement demand that scholarly systems facilitate traceability. This entails not only recognising authorship but linking it persistently to a verifiable identity. In the absence of such a structure, marginalised scholars are disproportionately excluded from intellectual credit, and exploitative citational practices—such as idea laundering or uncredited synthesis—become widespread~\cite{mott2019citation}. Moreover, anonymity shields not only honest critics but also intellectual bad actors, enabling trolling, plagiarism, and the untraceable distortion of discourse~\cite{esposito2017transparency}.

Blockchain-based identity systems enable immutable attribution and citation logs tied to real identities. By embedding authorship and commentary in an open, append-only record, these technologies offer a structural reconfiguration of scholarly ethics~\cite{roberts2019blockchainedu}. They allow for both praise and critique to be contextualised and credited properly, moving acknowledgement from a marginal, symbolic gesture to a core function of epistemic integrity~\cite{tseng2020blockchainpedagogy}. Such systems do not merely document contributions—they honour them, enforcing the moral premise that recognition must follow creation.




\section{Conclusion}

The structure of academic knowledge cannot remain beholden to an antiquated model of static publication, anonymous gatekeeping, and disciplinary closure. In this work, we have proposed a system that integrates identity, traceability, and public accountability into every stage of scholarly engagement. The review process must not culminate in silent approval or hidden rejection, but extend into a continual and verifiable conversation. Citations, revisions, commentary, and correction should all persist as linked expressions of ongoing thought.

This model demands identity-linked contributions, where each addition, critique, or amendment enhances rather than conceals the provenance of knowledge. Instead of privileging anonymity and deferral to status, the system privileges work that endures under scrutiny, is replicated, adapted, and expanded. Intellectual labour is honoured not by arbitrary thresholds of publication but by its integration, responsiveness, and visible effect across domains.

The future of research is not one of guarded silos but open, federated commentary. We envision a scholarly environment where knowledge is not judged by credentials but refined through action, use, and adaptation. Dialogue, contradiction, and correction are not signs of failure but hallmarks of progress. The system must recognise these acts not as peripheral but central—transforming scholarship from static archive to dynamic epistemic infrastructure.
\subsection{Towards a Federated, Transparent, and Active Intellectual Ecosystem}

The intellectual ecosystem of the future must reject centralised arbiters of academic value in favour of a federated architecture where authority is derived from verifiable contribution and continual interaction. In this model, transparency is structural: every comment, citation, replication, and revision is traceable to a real identity, embedded in an append-only, public ledger. Institutions no longer dictate legitimacy; instead, legitimacy is earned through sustained participation and epistemic resilience.

This federated design distributes the processes of review, validation, and synthesis across a network of actors—human and algorithmic—whose identities and histories of contribution are linked. Scholarly value ceases to be static; it emerges dynamically from usage, integration, contradiction, and corroboration. Public commentary replaces closed-door appraisal. Reviews are not ephemeral judgments but living annotations, forming part of the record.

Such a system does not merely accelerate the dissemination of knowledge—it redefines it. Research becomes an active process, not an endpoint. Instead of a paper serving as a monolithic claim, it becomes a node in a continuous dialogue, shaped and reshaped by its engagement. Through this, the intellectual landscape becomes not a gated archive but a responsive, evolving structure of interconnected thought. Transparency is not a virtue—it is the medium itself.```
\subsection{Knowledge as Conversation, Not Credential}

Credentialism reduces epistemic value to institutional affiliation, rank, and publication metrics, entrenching status hierarchies at the expense of argument quality and empirical robustness. This model mistakes origin for merit and decorates authority rather than testing claims. By contrast, a conversational model of knowledge positions each contribution as a response—explicit or implicit—to an ongoing inquiry. Validity emerges not from position but from engagement, coherence, replication, and utility.

In this reframed ecosystem, knowledge is dialogical. Each paper, citation, commentary, or correction is a turn in an intellectual conversation, bound not by deference to title but by epistemic accountability and public traceability. Dialogue replaces doctrine. Value arises not from exclusion, but from the visible interrelation of thought: criticism, confirmation, reinterpretation.

Credential becomes merely one data point in a larger network of actions and ideas. What matters is not who you are, but what you build, challenge, correct, and refine. This vision demotes passive authority and elevates dynamic contribution. It fosters a scholarly culture where truth is pursued collectively, transparently, and actively—through conversation, not certification.




