\subsection{Multiscale based on Shepard approximation with noisy data} \label{Sc-3-1}
Following~\cite{sharon2022multiscale}, we compare the traditional Shepard approximation~\cite{shepard1968two} with its multiscale version. Given a dataset~$X = \{(x_i, f_i) \}_{i=1}^N$, the Shepard approximation at a point~$s$ is computed as follows:
%\begin{equation*}
 $  \nicefrac{\sum_{i=1}^N f_i K(r_i)} {\sum_{i=1}^N  K(r_i)}$,
%\end{equation*}
where $r_i = \delta^{-1} \|x_i - s\|_2$ and $\delta$ is defined as in Section~\ref{Sc-3-3}. We use the radial kernel~$K(r) = (\max\{1-r, 0 \})^4 \cdot (4r+1)$ from~\cite{wendland2004scattered}.

We analyze the performances over the target function~\eqref{Eq-3-1}, which has a grid-like wave pattern. This pattern generates high local bias. The function is
\begin{equation} \label{Eq-3-1}
    f(x,y) = \sin(2\pi x)\cos(4\pi y)(1 + \nu\cdot p).   
\end{equation}
Here, the noise term~$\nu$ is sampled from~$\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ at every evaluation of the target function, and the constant~$p$ acts as the standard deviation of the Gaussian noise. In each trial, we sample approximately $14^2$ uniformly random points from $[0,1]^2$. 

Figure~\ref{FigN-4-1} illustrates the comparison of Shepard (singlescale) and its multiscale for different signal-to-noise ratios (SNR), $\operatorname{SNR} = {1}/{p^2}$. Figure~\ref{FigN-4-1-sub1} demonstrates a lower bias ratio for the multiscale for any SNR. In addition, Figure~\ref{FigN-4-1-sub2} supports several past works, the multiscale suggests a preferable error rate. Specifically, the multiscale results in lower error rates, as indicated by the median and tighter 25th and 75th percentiles, for $ \operatorname {SNR} > 0.5$. However, for sufficiently high noise levels, both methods exhibit similar error rates. The similar error rates suggest Shepard performs well, leaving little room for multiscale improvement.
