    \label{FigN-4-1-sub2}
\end{subfigure}%
\caption{Comparing Shepard (red) and its multiscale (blue) across different noise-to-signal ratios. The target is a noisy scalar-valued function with a pattern that generates high local bias~\eqref{Eq-3-1}.}
\label{FigN-4-1}
\end{figure}
\vspace{-10pt}

\subsection{Approximating function with values on a Hadamard manifold}
We extend our previous experiment into a symmetric positive definite (SPD) manifold setting, which is a special case of a Hadamard manifold. We define the multiscale operators over the manifold according to~\cite{sharon2022multiscale}.
The target function and the noise are defined in the tangent space, symmetric matrices, and projected to the SPD manifold:
\begin{equation}\label{Eq-3-2}
    G(x,y) = \exp_\M\left(A(x,y) 
    (I + \Sigma\cdot p)\right), 
\end{equation}
where $A(x,y)= \begin{pmatrix}
    \sin(2\pi y)\cos(2\pi x) & y^2 & xy\\
    y^2 &  1 & 0 \\ xy & 0 & \cos(\pi x)
    \end{pmatrix}$.
%\begin{equation*}
%    A(x,y)= \begin{pmatrix}
%    \sin(2\pi y)\cos(2\pi x) & y^2 & xy\\
%    y^2 &  1 & 0 \\ xy & 0 & \cos(\pi x)
%    \end{pmatrix}.
%\end{equation*}
Here, $\exp_\M$ is the matrix exponential, $\Sigma$ is a symmetric matrix whose entries are sampled independently and identically from $ \mathcal {N}(0,1)$, and $ p$ represents a predetermined noise constant. In this case, we do not have an analytical expression for the SNR. Instead, we use the numerical mean of~$\operatorname{SNR}(x,y) = {\norm{A(x,y)}_{F}^2}/{\norm{A(x,y) \cdot \Sigma}_{F}^2}$ over~$[0,1]^2$ where $\norm{\cdot}_F$ is the Frobenius norm. The dataset consists of approximately $11^2$ points, which are sampled uniformly at random from the square $ [0,1]^2$.

Based on the version of the BVT to manifold~\eqref{Eq-2-6}-\eqref{Eq-2-2-5}, we compare the Shepard approximation and its multiscale version. As in the previous examples, in Figure~\ref{FigN-4-3-sub1}, the multiscale demonstrates a significantly lower bias ratio. In Figure~\ref{FigN-4-3-sub2}, the multiscale suggests superior error rates. %We note that in a low SNR setting, the multiscale might fail due to highly ill-conditioned matrices.
