\begin{comment}
\section{Conclusion}

This work investigates a  property of the multiscale approximation—its lower bias ratio—through numerical experiments.
Along with the multiscale method’s superior error rate, this property helps mitigate issues such as highly smooth approximation and suboptimal choices of hyper-parameters.
Additionally, we examine this property across various types of target functions, such as manifold-valued functions, and with different approximation operators.
We explore this property using a simple yet effective measurement~\eqref{Eq-2-3-1}, the bias ratio, which could be valuable for future research.

This work offers a numerical perspective on the multiscale structure and its properties. Future research could focus on developing theoretical foundations to support our numerical results. Another promising direction involves extending the current framework to encompass broader classes of manifolds. Additionally, exploring how the decaying bias ratio influences advanced applications—such as dataset representation and learning~\cite{rabin2012heterogeneous}, or model order reduction via manifold approximation~\cite{farhat2020computational,zimmermann2021manifold}—could yield valuable insights.
\end{comment}

