\subsection{Landauer Bound in Non-Markovian Process}

%For a complete description of the quantum system, one needs to include the impact of the environment. The study of the dynamics of the system that interacts with such environments is described as an open quantum system (OQS)~\cite{breuer2002theory,rivas2012open,rotter2015review,viola1999dynamical,breuer2016colloquium,de2017dynamics,mukhopadhyay2017dynamics,bhattacharya2017exact}. It plays a crucial role in many aspects, like quantum information technology where the system which carries the information is affected by the surrounding noise~\cite{breuer2002theory}. 
The Markovian approximation is the most convenient way to express the dynamics of OQS. In this process, the evolution timescale is considered to be larger than the correlation time of the environment. In other words, in this approximation, the memory effect (or information backflow) is neglected. However, it plays a non-minuscule role in the analysis of the dynamics of the system and thermodynamic cost. There comes the role of non-Markovian (NM) dynamics to explore the dynamics of the system~\cite{de2017dynamics,breuer2016colloquium}. Various methods and approaches have surfaced for defining as well as quantifying non-Makovianity~\cite{breuer2009measure,rivas2010entanglement,chruscinski2014degree}. %So far, we have explored LP under Markovian dynamics. Here we study the LP for a system undergoing an NM process. %The details of the OQS are out of the scope of this review, interested readers can look into the reviews~\cite{de2017dynamics,breuer2016colloquium} for details. 




Out of the various methods, one is the applicability of the collision model for NM dynamics~\cite{rau1963relaxation,alicki2007quantum,scarani2002thermalizing,ziman2005all,gennaro2009relaxation}. In the collision model, the NM dynamics for a system can be achieved when the interaction of the system and the environment is intervened by degrees of freedom of the ancilla. \bb{Under NM dynamics, where coherence endures and information retrogrades from the environment, a coherence-dependent correction to LB~\cite{reeb2014improved} becomes imperative. Unlike Markovian erasure, where dissipation follows a strict lower bound, NM effects can modify these limits, potentially reducing or amplifying heat dissipation.}
\bb{Here, we extend Landauer’s principle to NM dynamics, exploring how memory effects influence erasure costs and coherence-driven corrections. This provides a more comprehensive view of quantum erasure beyond the standard Markovian framework.}


The formulation of a Ladauer-like principle for heat flux in an erasure process delineated by the collision model of OQS is first proposed in~\cite{lorenzo2015landauer}. 
