\section{Skybox}
In Ref.~\cite{10.1093/ptep/ptx127}, a skydome is used for the Lorentz transformation of distant scenes. Here, we generalize it to an algorithm that employs a skybox $S$. This skybox, a virtual cubic enclosure around the player (observer, or camera), represents the distant scene.
We repurpose the environmental or reflection mapping algorithm, commonly used for specular reflection in graphic engines such as Unity, to project a Lorentz-transformed world onto the skybox $S$.


Let
\al{
\lv{X_\P}=\bmat{X_\P^0\\ \bs X_\P}\qquad\pn{\,=\mt L\fn{\bs u_\P}\lv{x_\P}\,}
}
be the player's position in the player's rest frame ($\bs u_\P$ is the player's velocity in the world frame at the moment). Recall that throughout this paper, the world frame and the (player's) rest frame are denoted by the coordinate systems $\lv x$ and $\lv X$.

We place the skybox $S$ in the player's rest frame.
For each point $\bs X_S$ of $S$ (which can be picked up as in the line *** in the sample code~\ref{}), we define the ``would-be line of sight''
\al{
\lv D:=\bmat{-\ab{\bs X_S-\bs X_\P}\\ \bs X_S-\bs X_\P},
}
where the time component is arranged such that the line-of-sight becomes a past-oriented light-like spacetime vector.
This vector is transformed to that in the world frame as
\al{
\lv d:=\mt L\fn{-\bs u_\P}\lv D;
}
see Eq.~\eqref{to rest frame} for the Lorentz transformation to the rest frame.

We prepare the true distant scene in the world frame.
Then the (normalized) spatial component $\bh d=\bs d/\ab{\bs d}$ can be used to pick up the direction (in the world frame) to be projected onto the point $\bs X_S$ (in the player's rest frame).
Specifically on Unity Engine, $\bh d$ can be used as \texttt{viewDir} in the following command:\footnote{
For our purpose, the ``Lattitude-Longitude Layout (Cylindrical)'' option, which uses the equirectangular projection, seems to work the best in texture mapping in Unity Engine.
}
\begin{quote}\begin{verbatim}
UNITY_SAMPLE_TEXCUBE(_Cube, viewDir);
\end{verbatim}\end{quote}
Here, we may use the skybox itself as the surface onto which the distant scene is projected.

