\subsection{Multi-System OMR}  Our starting point is the success of Sheet Music Transformer++ (SMT++; \citealp{SMT++}), which is, to our knowledge, the only model designed to handle multiple systems in a score rather than single-staff or single-system scores \citep{Olimpic, GrandStaff}.  SMT++ is an encoder-decoder transformer model trained end-to-end on purely synthetic, full-page piano-form scores. It was trained on FP-GrandStaff (688 pages), which  was generated by randomly concatenating single-system piano scores from GrandStaff~\citep{GrandStaff}. SMT++ is the baseline against which we compare our approach.


\subsection{Output Score Representation}  
Many different symbolic score formats have been considered in past work on OMR; this paper considers **kern, ABC, and MusicXML.

\textbf{**kern} is a musical representation designed %in the 1980s 
within the Humdrum toolkit~\citep{kern}. **kern is ASCII-based
%, and uses a repetition based pitch notation, where C4 is represented by \texttt{c}, C3 is represented as \texttt{C}, and successive lowercase or uppercase letters indicating octave shifts higher or lower respectively (i.e. C5 is \texttt{cc} and C2 is \texttt{CC}). Octave changes occur between C and B. Durations are modeled using integers, where a 1 indicates a whole note, an 8 indicates an eighth note, etc. Rests are explicitly modeled in the same manner using \texttt{r}. 
and places pitch and relative duration as the main focal points of the format, with visual information coming in second. **kern explicitly models concurrent notes using spaces for notes in the same staff, and tabs for notes in other staves. It was designed with music researchers in mind, and with some study can be fluently read by humans.
**kern is an OMR target in SMT++~\citep{SMT++} and other systems~\citep{SMT,GrandStaff}. 

The \textbf{ABC} music standard was introduced %in 1993 
as an alternative ASCII-based form of musical notation~\citep{abc}. 
%It is based on Helmholtz pitch notation, meaning that rather than the repetitious notation of **kern, \texttt{'} and \texttt{,} respectively indicate higher and lower octaves. The capital and lowercase notations are also shifted an octave from **kern --- \texttt{C} represents C4, and \texttt{c} represents C5, with \texttt{C,} reserved for C3. Each instance of \texttt{'} or \texttt{,} indicates an octave shift, meaning that C4 can be represented as \texttt{C} or \texttt{c,}; this is one reason that ABC notation is not strictly canonical.
Similar to **kern, it can be written and read by any text editor. 
% \vcomment{this could be removed? from here} Although it was initially designed for single-staff traditional Western European folk tunes, its use has expanded to more complex music types, with many staves, voices, and instruments. \vcomment{to here} 
The main attraction of ABC notation is the simple, concise format---a song that would take thousands of lines in other formats will take only tens in ABC, potentially reducing the computational cost for autoregressive models like our decoder. ABC notation can encode lyrics, title, tempo, decorations, articulations, and even some kinds of typesetting parameters, making it a nearly comprehensive format. % \nascomment{unlike **kern?} \guang{**kern can partially encode this info, but previous datasets didn't include them.}. 
The more explicit structure for musical engravings such as barlines and linebreaks, as well as a notation more similar to sheet music than that of **kern, %---e.g., a barline in **kern is represented with \texttt{=}, whereas  it is \texttt{|} in ABC---
has led to ABC notation as a target for many systems~\citep{melodyt5, abctext2music, abcmlm}, but
%has allowed ABC to become more widely used than **kern, and over  730K examples available for free download from medieval music to pop music.\footnote{\url{https://abcnotation.com/}} 
%For this reason, ABC notation is more widespread among non-research musicians. \vcomment{need to find a citation for this but it feels true.}
to our knowledge, image-to-ABC OMR models have not yet been trained.
% \guang{Some software can do monophonic ABC OMR, but it's a simplified MusicXML model + converter. Model is not trained on ABC.} 
% \nascomment{better now?}

\textbf{MusicXML} is a tree-based music notation format introduced in 2001~\citep{musicxml}, widely adopted by commercial software such as MuseScore~\citep{MuseScore} and Sibelius~\citep{Sibelius}. Its popularity has made it a common target for OMR and music transcription tasks~\citep{musicxmltranscription}, sometimes with simplifications~\citep{Olimpic}. However, compared to ABC and **kern, MusicXML is more verbose, harder to parse, and its hierarchical structure poses challenges for sequence models.

