The constraints on $H_0$ are listed in Table~\ref{tab:1}, and posteriors and corresponding credible intervals for $H_0$ are shown in Figure~\ref{Fig:3}. 
A direct measurement of $H_0$ using FRBs alone is difficult due to strong parameter degeneracies. Specifically, our measurement using FRBs alone is $H_0 = 77.0^{+20.0}_{-40.0}$ \kms, and the inclusion of GW data narrows the constraint to $H_0 = 71.6^{+4.4}_{-8.6}$ \kms. This improvement is mainly driven by the GW data itself, which yields $H_0 = 72.6^{+5.2}_{-8.9}$ \kms.
Previous studies predominantly inferred $H_0$ by fixing the baryon density parameter at the early-Universe value.
For example, \citet{Wu:2021jyk} (hereafter Wu22) and \citet{James:2022dcx} (hereafter James22) used different FRB samples to derive $H_0 = 69.31^{+6.21}_{-6.63}~\mathrm{km~s^{-1}~Mpc^{-1}}$ and $H_0 = 73^{+12}_{-8}~\mathrm{km~s^{-1}~Mpc^{-1}}$, by assuming external priors on $\Omega_{\rm b}h^2 \sim \mathcal{N}(0.02235, 0.00049)$ from BBN and $\Omega_{\rm b}h^2 = 0.02242$ from Planck18, respectively, which are shown as red and gray bands in Figure~\ref{Fig:3}.
