\title[hyperlinearity, stability and character rigidity]{Connections between hyperlinearity, stability and character rigidity for higher rank lattices}
\author{Alon Dogon and Itamar Vigdorovich}
%\date{\today}

% classification if needed.
\thanks{\textbf{2020 Mathematics Subject Classification:} Primary: 22E40, 20H05, 22D25, 20E26, 43A35. Secondary: 22D55, 46L53, 20C25}

\begin{document}


\begin{abstract}
Let $\Gamma$ be an irreducible lattice in a semisimple Lie group of real rank at least $2$.  Suppose that $\Gamma$ has property (T;FD), that is, its finite dimensional representations have a uniform  spectral gap. 
We show that if $\Gamma$ is (flexibly) Hilbert--Schmidt stable then: $(a)$ infinite central extensions $\widetilde{\Gamma}$ of $\Gamma$ are not hyperlinear, and $(b)$ every character of $\Gamma$ is either finite-dimensional or induced from the center (character rigidity).
As a consequence, a positive answer to the following question would yield an explicit example of a non-hyperlinear group: If two  representations of the modular group $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ almost agree on a specific congruence subgroup $H$ under a commensuration, must they be close to representations that genuinely agree on $H$?



    % Let $\Gamma$ be a group with property (T;FD), namely, there is a uniform spectral gap over all finite-dimensional representations, and assume that $\Gamma$ has an infinite central extension $\widetilde{\Gamma}$ with finite abelianization.
    % We show that if $\Gamma$ is (flexibly) Hilbert--Schmidt stable (roughly, every almost representation is close to a genuine one), then $\widetilde{\Gamma}$ is not hyperlinear.
