\paragraph*{Summary of SOD vs. cosine comparison.}
Taken together, these analyses consistently demonstrate a weak relationship between direct SOD scores and RIM cosine similarity across all dataset conditions. This relationship, characterized generally by weak negative rank correlation (on raw data) and low percentile agreement (after log transformation), becomes progressively weaker, approaching statistical orthogonality, as definitions undergo more specific curation (from ground-filtered to targeted removal). This trend strongly suggests that SOD captures distinct aspects of semantic relatedness grounded in explicit definitional structure, which are largely independent of the network proximity information captured by RIM cosine similarity, particularly when applied to a more curated, semantically focused network like the targeted removal dataset.

\subsection{Comparison of cumulative SOD for SN vs. SP paths}
\label{subsec:results_path_comparison}

Next, we evaluated the semantic coherence of paths generated by the SN and SP algorithms by comparing their cumulative SOD scores. For a given path $\mathcal{P}$, this score is defined as $\text{SOD}_C(\mathcal{P}) = \sum_{i=1}^{L} \text{SOD}(p_0, p_{i})_{\omega_{GOD}+1}$ (see Sec.~\ref{subsec:sod_eval}). This evaluation was performed across the ~2 million source-target pairs for each of the three dataset conditions. A lower $\text{SOD}_C$ score indicates greater definitional coherence relative to the path origin.


