\subsection{Model-Reduction: A Low Dimensional Dynamics}
%\subsubsection{Sparsity Methods for Model Reduction}


To realize a low-dimensional representation of the original discrete-time system \eqref{eq:DT-systems}, we project the dynamics onto a subspace spanned by a selected set of $m$ Koopman modes (e.g., see \cite{susuki2011nonlinear}) using sparsity-based selection, expressed as
\begin{align}
\bfg(\bfx_{k})\approx\sum_{j=1}^{m}\lambda_{j}^{t}\varphi_{j}(\bfx_{0})\bfv_{j}=\sum_{j=1}^{m}\eta_{j}[k]\bfv_{j}
    :=\bfV\bmeta[k],
    \label{eq:reduced-order-model}
\end{align}
where $\bmeta=[\eta_{1},\ldots,\eta_{m}]^{\top}\in{{\mathbb{C}}}^{m}$ and
$\bfV=[\bfv_{1},\ldots,\bfv_{m}]\in{\mathbb{C}}^{p\times{m}}$. By substituting \eqref{eq:reduced-order-model} into \eqref{eq:Koopman-decomp-output-3}, we have 
\begin{align}
    \sum_{j=1}^{m}\eta_{j}[k+1]\bfv_{j}=\big(\bfg\circ{\bfT}\circ\bfg^{-1}\big)(\bfV\bmeta[k]),
    \label{eq:reduced-order-model-2}
\end{align}
where the inverse of observable function $\bfg^{-1}$ exists because the data composed of observables is always available through direct measurements in climate systems, such as SST data.
%For the sake of brevity, we can take the observable $\bff$ to be the identity.

By multiplying both sides of \eqref{eq:reduced-order-model-2} by $\bfv_{i}^{\dag}$, we have 
\begin{align}
      \sum_{j=1}^{m}\eta_{j}[k+1]\bfv_{i}^{\dag}\bfv_{j}&=\underbrace{\bfv_{i}^{\dag}\big(\bfg\circ{\bfT}\circ\bfg^{-1}\big)(\bfV\bmeta[k])}_{:=R_{i}(\bmeta[k])}
    \label{eq:reduced-order-model-3}
\end{align}
for $i=1,2,\ldots,m$.
Now if we take $\widehat{\bfV}:=\bfV^{\dag}\bfV\in{\mathbb{C}}^{m\times{m}}$, it thus gives rise to a compact form of a low-dimensional linear system representation of \eqref{eq:reduced-order-model-3}, that is,
%representation of \eqref{eq:reduced-order-model-3}, that is,
\begin{align}
\widehat{\bfV}\eta[k+1]=\bfR(\bmeta[k]),
\label{eq:reduced-order-model-4}
\end{align}
where %$\widehat{\bfV}=\bfV^{\dag}\bfV\in{\mathbb{C}}^{m\times{m}}$, 
$\bfR(\bmeta):=[R_{1}(\bmeta),\ldots,R_{m}(\bmeta)]^{\top}\in{\mathbb{C}}^{m}$. This discrete time system \eqref{eq:reduced-order-model-3} or \eqref{eq:reduced-order-model-4} defined on ${\mathbb{C}}^{m}$ gives a \emph{reduced-order} 
system model of \eqref{eq:DT-systems} in terms of the first $m$ Koopman modes, which can be achieved through SPDMD \eqref{eq:regulaized-least-square-prob}.



