Suppose that there is no singular continuous spectrum. Then any (scalar) observable $g\in{\mathcal{L}}^{2}(\mathcal{M},\mu)$, it follows that 
\begin{align}
g=\sum_{\lambda_{j}\in\sigma(\mathcal{K})}\varphi_{j}\bfv_j+\int_{[-\pi,\pi]_{\text{per}}}\phi_{\theta,g}\der\theta
\end{align}
where $\sigma(\lambda)$ is the set of eigenvalues (i.e., spectrum) of $\mathcal{K}$, 
%$\varphi_{\lambda}$ are the eigenfunctions of $\mathcal{K}$, 
%$c_{\lambda}$ are expansion coefficients 
and $\phi_{\theta,g}$ is a ``continuously parameterized'' collection of eigenfunctions \cite[Sec.~2]{colbrook2024rigorous}.
%, which shows the density of absolutely continuous part  $\rho_{g}=\langle\,\phi_{\theta,g},g\rangle$. 
%The second term in the RHS is continuous spectra.
Based on \eqref{eq:Koopman-decomp-output-3}, the KMD can be further defined as \cite{mezic2005spectral}
\begin{align*}
    g(\bfx_{t})&={\mathcal{K}}^{t}(g(\bfx_{0}))\\
&=\mathop{\sum}\limits_{\lambda_{j}\in\sigma(\mathcal{K})}\lambda_{j}^{t}\varphi_{j}(\bfx_{0})\bfv_{j} 
   +\int_{[-\pi,\pi]_{\text{per}}}e^{in\theta}\phi_{\theta,g}(\bfx_{0})\der\theta.
\end{align*}

