For a finite matrix $\bfA$ and $\epsilon>0$, the $\epsilon$-pseudospectrum of $\bfA$ is defined as
 \begin{align*}
  \sigma_{\epsilon}(\bfA)&=\left\{\lambda\in{\mathbb{C}}:\|(\bfA-\lambda{I})^{-1}\|\geq{1}/\epsilon\right\}\\
  &=\cup_{\|\bfB\|\leq\epsilon}\sigma(\bfA+\bfB).
 \end{align*}
It should be noted that if we replace $\bfA$ with the Koopman operator $\mathcal{K}$, we need consider the closure of the set for $\sigma_{\epsilon}(\mathcal{K})$, as the Koopman operator may be an \emph{unbounded} operator.

Given the grids $z_1,z_2,\ldots,z_k\in{\mathbb{C}}$, one can approximate $\epsilon$-pseudospectrum 
  $\{z_j:\tau_j<\epsilon\}$ and approximiate the eigenfunction $\{\varpi_j:\tau_j<\epsilon\}$ such that 
  $\bfA\varpi_j=\lambda_j\varpi_j$, where $\tau_j$ can be viewed as the resudials. 
As mentioned in \cite{colbrook2024rigorous}, residual DMD is a powerful tool for computing the \emph{pseudo-spectrum} and \emph{continuous spectrum} based on EDMD and kernelized EDMD via the Galerkin method; see also the references therein for detailed algorithms.

